window.QUIZ_DATA = {"course":"Pharmacology for Nurses","version":1,"lessons":[{"id":"L1","title":"What Is a Drug?","sub":"Introduction, drug sources and drug names","emoji":"💊","slides":[1,35],"recap":["Pharmacology studies the two-way interaction between drugs and the body: pharmacokinetics (what the body does to the drug) and pharmacodynamics (what the drug does to the body).","Clinically a drug is a chemical used to prevent, diagnose or treat disease. The same chemical turns into a poison with an overdose, long use or the wrong route.","Drugs come from six sources: natural, synthetic, semisynthetic, genetic engineering, immunological and serological.","A drug has a chemical, a generic and a brand name. Prescriptions normally use the generic name; the brand name is for when quality assurance matters."],"questions":[{"id":"L1-bxg8fy","slide":9,"slides":[9],"q":"The word pharmacology is built from two Greek roots. What do they mean?","o":["Pharmakos (a healer) and nomos (a rule or law of practice)","Pharmakeia (a pharmacy) and techne (a craft or skill)","Pharmakon (a drug) and logos (knowledge or discourse)","Pharmakon (a poison) and graphe (a written record)"],"a":2,"e":"Pharmakon means drug and logos means knowledge, so pharmacology is all knowledge about drugs."},{"id":"L1-3dk4gd","slide":9,"slides":[9],"q":"Which statement best captures pharmacology as the lecturer defines it?","o":["The science of compounding and dispensing medicines","The science of how drugs act on living systems","The study of how diseases arise in living systems","The study of how medicines are tested and priced"],"a":1,"e":"Pharmacology is the science of the action of drugs on a biological system, the body."},{"id":"L1-1jnia6y","slide":10,"slides":[10],"q":"Why does the lecture call pharmacology the study of an 'interaction'?","o":["Drugs interact only with other drugs given at the same time","Only substances the body makes itself are ever studied there","The drug affects the body, and the body affects the drug","The body acts on the drug but the drug never changes the body"],"a":2,"e":"The interaction is mutual: drug effect on the body and body effect on the drug."},{"id":"L1-1e1sfiq","slide":11,"slides":[11],"q":"A drug is absorbed from the gut, bound to albumin, metabolised by the liver and excreted in urine. Which division of pharmacology describes this?","o":["Pharmacodynamics, the way drugs affect the body","Pharmacognosy, the way drugs are sourced from nature","Pharmacokinetics, the way the body handles drugs","Toxicology, the way poisons are detected and treated"],"a":2,"e":"Pharmacokinetics covers the ADME handling of a drug by the body; pharmacodynamics covers mechanism of action."},{"id":"L1-3gjyrx","slide":12,"slides":[12],"q":"Two patients take the same dose of one drug but respond very differently. Which basic principle explains this?","o":["Biotransformation is the same thing as detoxification","Drug response varies widely from person to person","Drugs of similar structure share an identical potency","Every drug has a single active principle and effect"],"a":1,"e":"One of the lecturer's basic principles is that drug response varies from person to person."},{"id":"L1-1ct0141","slide":12,"slides":[12],"q":"Two drugs have very similar chemical structures. What does the lecture predict?","o":["Identical effects and identical potency in every patient","Similar effects, though their potency may differ","Opposite effects because each binds a different receptor","Similar potency but completely unrelated effects"],"a":1,"e":"Drugs of similar structure give similar effects but with different potency."},{"id":"L1-qrvrrk","slide":12,"slides":[12],"q":"Which statement about how drugs produce effects fits the lecture's basic principles?","o":["Most drugs bind receptors, and effect varies with dose","Most drugs dissolve the cell membrane, whatever the dose","Effect depends on receptor binding but never on the dose","Effect is fixed once any amount of the drug is present"],"a":0,"e":"Most drugs bind receptors, and there is a relationship between dose and effect."},{"id":"L1-k711mb","slide":12,"slides":[12],"q":"An active principle in a drug can give rise to which effects?","o":["Only therapeutic effects, whatever the dose given","Therapeutic and placebo effects but never toxic ones","Only side and toxic effects, never therapeutic ones","Therapeutic, side and toxic effects depending on dose"],"a":3,"e":"The active principle is responsible for therapeutic, side and toxic effects."},{"id":"L1-8htkdp","slide":13,"slides":[13],"q":"Why is biotransformation NOT the same as detoxification?","o":["Metabolism always yields a metabolite more toxic than its parent","Metabolism can turn an inert prodrug into an active drug","Metabolism begins only after the drug has been excreted","Detoxification occurs in the kidney, metabolism only in the liver"],"a":1,"e":"Inert parent drugs (prodrugs) become active after metabolism, so metabolism is not always detoxification."},{"id":"L1-kd701z","slide":13,"slides":[13],"q":"The group name 'antihypertensives' tells a student the group's:","o":["Shared chemical structure across every drug in the group","Shared receptor target across every drug in the group","Shared route of administration for every drug in the group","Reason for existing: managing high blood pressure"],"a":3,"e":"A group name is paired with the reason for its existence: antihypertensives manage hypertension."},{"id":"L1-ni3jk","slide":14,"slides":[14],"q":"In the lecturer's plan for studying a drug group, what is a prototype?","o":["A representative drug used to study the whole group","The very first formulation ever marketed in that group","The one drug in the class that has no side effects","A trial batch made before the drug is licensed"],"a":0,"e":"The prototype or representative of the group is studied to learn the features of the whole group."},{"id":"L1-65clro","slide":15,"slides":[15],"q":"Which item is NOT part of the lecturer's general plan for studying a drug?","o":["Sales history of the drug's manufacturer","Undesired effects and toxicity of the drug","Contraindications for particular patient groups","Drug-drug interactions with other medicines"],"a":0,"e":"The plan covers uses, undesired effects, toxicity, contraindications and drug-drug interactions."},{"id":"L1-14du02j","slide":16,"slides":[16],"q":"The English word 'drug' comes from Dutch and French words meaning what?","o":["'Healing water', since early drugs came from mineral springs","'Pure powder', since early drugs were ground minerals","'Strong potion', since early drugs were fermented drinks","'Dry' and 'dry herb', since early drugs were dried plants"],"a":3,"e":"Droog (Dutch) means dry and drogue (French) means dry herb; early drugs were dried plants."},{"id":"L1-2pa94w","slide":16,"slides":[16],"q":"The lecture places antibiotics under which definition of a drug?","o":["Chemicals toxic to organisms that invade the body","Chemicals that alter physiological processes of the body","Substances that the body synthesises and releases itself","Substances found only in dried plant material"],"a":0,"e":"The second definition covers chemicals with toxic effects on invading organisms, e.g. antibiotics."},{"id":"L1-liyfla","slide":17,"slides":[17],"q":"Which description fits a xenobiotic?","o":["A substance the body makes but releases in unusually high amounts","A substance that is harmful only to invading microorganisms","A substance the body does not make, introduced from outside","A substance the body stores only in fat and bone tissue"],"a":2,"e":"Xenobiotics are not synthesised by the body but come from outside, so they are foreign to it."},{"id":"L1-1xvk3i5","slide":17,"slides":[17],"q":"Which of these would the lecture's broad definition call a drug?","o":["Glucose released by the person's own liver during fasting","Caffeine in tea, since it enters from outside the body","Insulin secreted by the pancreas after a normal meal","Adrenaline released by the person's own adrenal glands"],"a":1,"e":"Food additives, caffeine, nicotine, alcohol and cosmetics are included because they are xenobiotics."},{"id":"L1-1sw5o8p","slide":18,"slides":[18],"q":"When does a hormone count as a drug in the lecture's definition?","o":["Only when it has been synthesised in a chemistry laboratory","When injected or released in excess by a stimulating agent","Whenever it circulates in the blood at normal levels","Only when it has been extracted from animal glands"],"a":1,"e":"Hormones count when given exogenously or released endogenously in large amounts by stimulating agents."},{"id":"L1-5gbg9u","slide":18,"slides":[18],"q":"How does the lecture relate poisons to drugs?","o":["Poisons form a separate class that can never act as drugs","Poisons are drugs only when taken with suicidal intent","Poisons are also drugs, taken by accident or deliberately","Poisons are drugs only when they are swallowed by mistake"],"a":2,"e":"Poisons are also drugs, whether taken accidentally or with suicidal intentions."},{"id":"L1-cwrrww","slide":20,"slides":[20],"q":"Which is NOT one of the ways a drug becomes a poison?","o":["Using the usual dose by the prescribed route","Giving an excessive dose of the drug","Using the usual dose for a very long time","Giving the usual dose by the wrong route"],"a":0,"e":"A drug becomes toxic with an overdose, long-term use of a therapeutic dose, or the wrong route."},{"id":"L1-18nz0f7","slide":21,"slides":[21],"q":"What most clearly separates a poison from an ordinary drug?","o":["A poison always comes from plants and animals","A poison can never be absorbed into the circulation","A poison acts on one organ and never on the brain","A poison has a very small or no safe dose range"],"a":3,"e":"Drugs have a safety dose range; poisons have an extremely small or no safety range."},{"id":"L1-17l7n","slide":22,"slides":[22],"q":"Which wording matches the clinical definition of a drug?","o":["A chemical used to prevent, monitor or eradicate disease","A chemical used to diagnose, treat or rehabilitate disease","A chemical used to prevent, isolate or report disease","A chemical used to prevent, diagnose or treat disease"],"a":3,"e":"Clinically a drug is a chemical substance used to prevent, diagnose or treat disease."},{"id":"L1-1rnobzd","slide":23,"slides":[23],"q":"Which of these is NOT one of the six sources of drugs listed in the lecture?","o":["Serological","Radiological","Immunological","Semisynthetic"],"a":1,"e":"The six sources are natural, synthetic, semisynthetic, genetic engineered, immunological and serological."},{"id":"L1-1e8ccyd","slide":24,"slides":[24],"q":"Which pair of drugs is obtained from plants?","o":["Insulin and morphine","Morphine and reserpine","Heparin and reserpine","Heparin and artemether"],"a":1,"e":"Morphine, reserpine and artemether are plant-derived; insulin and heparin come from animals."},{"id":"L1-cokx8b","slide":24,"slides":[24],"q":"Liquid paraffin, magnesium and calcium salts illustrate which natural source?","o":["Animals, which are biological natural sources","Microorganisms, which are biological natural sources","Plants, which are biological natural sources","Minerals, which are non-biological natural sources"],"a":3,"e":"Minerals are the non-biological natural source (Mg, Ca, SO4, liquid paraffin)."},{"id":"L1-6i7qqm","slide":24,"slides":[24],"q":"Which natural sources supply heparin and antibiotics, in that order?","o":["Microorganisms and animals","Plants and microorganisms","Animals and microorganisms","Minerals and animals"],"a":2,"e":"Heparin comes from animals; antibiotics come from microorganisms."},{"id":"L1-1vjhnnd","slide":25,"slides":[25],"q":"Which of these is wholly manufactured, a synthetic drug?","o":["Morphine","Procaine","Heparin","Reserpine"],"a":1,"e":"Sulphonamides, procaine and corticosteroids are wholly synthetic."},{"id":"L1-17b019x","slide":26,"slides":[26],"q":"Why is co-amoxiclav called a semisynthetic product?","o":["It is made by inserting human genes into animals","It combines a natural product with a synthetic one","It is extracted whole from a mould in the laboratory","It is prepared from an antigen to stimulate antibodies"],"a":1,"e":"Amoxicillin (natural aminopenicillin) is combined with synthetic clavulanic acid."},{"id":"L1-w337sz","slide":27,"slides":[27],"q":"How is human insulin made by genetic engineering in the lecture's example?","o":["An animal insulin gene is placed in the human genome","Pancreas tissue is ground up and purified directly","Antibodies are raised against insulin in an animal","The human insulin gene is put into an animal genome"],"a":3,"e":"Recombinant DNA technique: the human gene is inserted into an animal, which then makes human insulin."},{"id":"L1-2te4wr","slide":28,"slides":[28],"q":"Vaccines and snake antivenoms belong to which two source categories, in that order?","o":["Serological, then immunological","Immunological, then genetic engineered","Natural, then semisynthetic","Immunological, then serological"],"a":3,"e":"Vaccines (antigens leading to antibodies) are immunological; antivenoms are serological."},{"id":"L1-1plxjsh","slide":29,"slides":[29],"q":"Most drugs are chemically best described as:","o":["Strong acids or strong bases","Weak acids or weak bases","Neutral, non-polar lipids","Permanently charged ions"],"a":1,"e":"Most drugs are weak acids or weak bases, so pH affects how ionised they are."},{"id":"L1-10q01gr","slide":29,"slides":[29],"q":"Why does the pH of a body fluid compartment matter for a drug?","o":["It changes the drug's molecular size in that fluid","It decides whether the drug counts as a poison","It switches the drug's receptor from one type to another","It changes the degree of ionisation and so the kinetics"],"a":3,"e":"Different pH values alter ionisation, which influences pharmacokinetic behaviour."},{"id":"L1-57hcyd","slide":30,"slides":[30],"q":"A drug generally has how many names, and which are they?","o":["Two: a generic and a brand name only","Three: chemical, generic and brand names","Four: chemical, generic, brand and local","Three: chemical, generic and clinical names"],"a":1,"e":"A drug generally has a chemical, a generic (non-proprietary) and a brand (proprietary) name."},{"id":"L1-y72tep","slide":31,"slides":[31],"q":"The long name for sildenafil beginning '5-[2-ethoxy-5-(4-methylpiperazin-1-ylsulfonyl)...' is its:","o":["Generic name, the one written on prescriptions","Chemical name, giving its exact composition","Brand name, owned by the manufacturer","Approved name, listed in the pharmacopoeia"],"a":1,"e":"The chemical name gives the precise chemical composition of the compound."},{"id":"L1-1lfwxou","slide":32,"slides":[32],"q":"Which statement about generic names is correct?","o":["Each manufacturer may register its own generic name","Generic names are identical in every country","A drug can have only one generic name in a country","Generic names are chosen to be catchy for marketing"],"a":2,"e":"Generic names are approved by national committees, and a country has only one for each drug."},{"id":"L1-1syll0u","slide":32,"slides":[32],"q":"How do common roots or endings in generic names help a clinician?","o":["They show which company manufactures the drug","They reveal the exact dose to prescribe","They give clues to the drug's use or group","They prove the drug has been approved abroad"],"a":2,"e":"Generic names share roots or endings that hint at the drug's use."},{"id":"L1-6mum6k","slide":32,"slides":[32],"q":"Where are generic names officially recorded after approval by national committees?","o":["In the national pharmacopoeia of that country","In the manufacturer's trademark register","In the wholesaler's annual price catalogue","In the patient information leaflet only"],"a":0,"e":"Generic names are approved nationally and documented in the national Pharmacopoeia."},{"id":"L1-y0k5vw","slide":33,"slides":[33],"q":"What is a drawback of generic names across borders?","o":["A drug's generic name may differ from country to country","They always cost more than the equivalent brand versions","Their quality is never assured anywhere","They cannot be written on hospital prescriptions"],"a":0,"e":"The lecture lists differences between countries as the disadvantage of generic names."},{"id":"L1-rs86z7","slide":33,"slides":[33],"q":"Viagra, for sildenafil citrate, is an example of which type of name?","o":["Generic name, approved nationally","Chemical name, showing its structure","Official name, listed in the pharmacopoeia","Brand name, a manufacturer's trademark"],"a":3,"e":"A brand (proprietary) name belongs to a manufacturing company, like a trademark."},{"id":"L1-pz1zpp","slide":33,"slides":[33],"q":"Which is an advantage of a brand name?","o":["It is cheaper than the generic version in most cases","Quality is almost always assured and the name is catchy","It is the only name accepted by national pharmacopoeias","It is identical in every country where it is sold"],"a":1,"e":"Brand names are easy to remember and quality is almost always assured."},{"id":"L1-xojyix","slide":34,"slides":[34],"q":"When does the lecture say a brand name should be written on a prescription?","o":["Always, because brand names are the official names","Only when you must be sure of the medicine's quality","Only when the generic name is longer than the brand","Whenever the patient is a child or elderly person"],"a":1,"e":"Prescriptions use generic names; brands are used only when we want to be sure of quality."},{"id":"L1-ueaz1u","slide":34,"slides":[34],"q":"Why can one drug carry several brand names in the same country?","o":["Each national committee approves a different name for it","Each pharmacy is allowed to rename it for customers","Each manufacturer sells it under its own trademark","Each new batch of the drug has to receive a new name"],"a":2,"e":"Several manufacturing companies mean several brand names for the same drug."},{"id":"L1-pwukk7","slide":35,"slides":[35],"q":"Barium meal and edrophonium are used mainly for which purpose?","o":["Preventing disease in healthy, vaccinated people","Controlling fertility for social reasons","Recreation without any medical purpose","Diagnosing disease rather than treating it"],"a":3,"e":"Both are listed as diagnostic uses of drugs."},{"id":"L1-1i2oqqk","slide":35,"slides":[35],"q":"Antifertility drugs used for birth control illustrate which use of drugs?","o":["Socio-economic expediency, not treating a disease","Prevention of infectious disease through immunisation","Recreational use for enjoyment or relaxation","Diagnosis of a hidden condition before treatment"],"a":0,"e":"Drugs for birth control are given as socio-economic expediency."},{"id":"L1-1bgu1mp","slide":35,"slides":[35],"q":"Which pair is correctly matched with its use?","o":["Vaccines for diagnosis; alcohol for recreation","Vaccines for prevention; alcohol for recreation","Vaccines for prevention; barium meal for birth control","Edrophonium for prevention; marijuana for treatment"],"a":1,"e":"Vaccines prevent disease; marijuana and alcohol are recreational uses."}]},{"id":"L2","title":"How Drugs Are Absorbed","sub":"ADME, bioavailability, membrane transport and absorption factors","emoji":"🧪","slides":[36,84],"recap":["Pharmacokinetics is ADME: absorption into plasma, distribution to tissues, metabolism to more polar compounds, and excretion. Bioavailability (F) is the fraction of a dose that reaches the circulation unchanged.","Passive diffusion is the most important way across membranes. Lipid-soluble, non-ionised drug crosses best; weak acids and bases depend on drug pKa and local pH (Henderson-Hasselbalch).","Other mechanisms: bulk flow (pressure-driven, important after SC/IM), active transport (energy, saturable, competitive), facilitated transport (no energy) and endocytosis (large molecules).","Absorption rises with dose, lipid solubility, small size, large surface area, good blood flow and liquid formulations."],"questions":[{"id":"L2-1lhq5d2","slide":36,"slides":[36],"q":"A drug leaves the bloodstream and enters the tissues. Which ADME process is this?","o":["Absorption, movement from the administration site to blood","Metabolism, conversion into more polar compounds","Distribution, movement from circulation to the tissues","Excretion, removal of drug molecules from the body"],"a":2,"e":"Distribution is the movement of drug molecules from circulation to tissues."},{"id":"L2-fk5edk","slide":36,"slides":[36],"q":"Which ADME process moves a drug from its site of administration into the circulation?","o":["Absorption","Distribution","Metabolism","Excretion"],"a":0,"e":"Absorption is the movement of drug molecules from the site of administration into circulation."},{"id":"L2-h4j2jr","slide":37,"slides":[37],"q":"Which description matches metabolism in pharmacokinetics?","o":["Movement of a drug from circulation into the tissues","Passage of a drug from the gut into the plasma","Removal of a drug by renal or biliary processes","Conversion of a drug to more polar compounds"],"a":3,"e":"Metabolism converts the drug into more polar compounds; excretion removes the drug from the body."},{"id":"L2-14rnr5c","slide":37,"slides":[37],"q":"Which routes are named for drug excretion in the lecture?","o":["Renal, biliary or other processes","Hepatic and plasma processes only","Gastric and enzymatic processes only","Absorptive and distributive processes"],"a":0,"e":"Excretion is elimination of drug molecules via renal, biliary or other processes."},{"id":"L2-wk79rs","slide":38,"slides":[38],"q":"In the ADME diagram, which organs are shown as sites of metabolism?","o":["Liver, lung and GI tract","Kidney, expired air, sweat and faeces","Body fat and other tissue depots","Cell receptors at the site of action"],"a":0,"e":"The diagram lists liver, lung and GI tract for metabolism; kidney, air, sweat and faeces for excretion."},{"id":"L2-89t9qs","slide":38,"slides":[38],"q":"In the ADME diagram, drug in plasma is divided into which two forms?","o":["Bound drug and free drug","Ionised drug and metabolised drug","Stored drug and excreted drug","Absorbed drug and distributed drug"],"a":0,"e":"The plasma compartment holds bound and free drug, in equilibrium."},{"id":"L2-12u3wro","slide":39,"slides":[39],"q":"Why is the effect of a drug more closely related to its plasma concentration than to the dose given?","o":["Absorption and other steps set how much reaches plasma","The dose never influences plasma concentration, because the liver caps it","Every patient reaches the same plasma level on any given dose","Plasma concentration is set only by the drug's brand and packaging"],"a":0,"e":"Effect (therapeutic or toxic) follows plasma concentration, which depends on how much is absorbed."},{"id":"L2-46r0nn","slide":40,"slides":[40],"q":"If the rate of absorption of a drug changes, which aspects of its action are influenced?","o":["Only the duration, because onset is fixed","Only the intensity, because onset is fixed","None, as long as the same dose is given","Onset, duration and intensity"],"a":3,"e":"Onset, duration and intensity of action are all influenced by the rate of absorption."},{"id":"L2-qetauy","slide":40,"slides":[40],"q":"Why might a change in absorption rate force a clinician to adjust treatment?","o":["The drug's generic name changes whenever absorption changes","Absorption alters the receptor subtype that the drug binds to","Plasma levels shift, so the dose may need adjusting","A faster rate always makes a drug safer for the patient"],"a":2,"e":"Changes in the rate of absorption may necessitate adjustment of the dose."},{"id":"L2-na8vxg","slide":41,"slides":[41],"q":"Bioavailability (F) is best defined as:","o":["The fraction of the dose reaching blood unchanged","The fraction of a dose excreted unchanged in the urine","The fraction of a dose bound to plasma proteins in blood","The fraction of a dose metabolised by the liver on first pass"],"a":0,"e":"F is the fraction of an administered drug that gets into the blood in its unchanged form."},{"id":"L2-14sh2l6","slide":41,"slides":[41],"q":"An oral tablet has a bioavailability of 0.4. What does this mean?","o":["40% of the dose is lost in the faeces without being absorbed","The drug starts to work 40 minutes after it is swallowed","40% of the dose reaches the circulation unchanged","The drug is 40% bound to plasma albumin after absorption"],"a":2,"e":"F = 0.4 means 40% of the administered dose enters plasma in unchanged form."},{"id":"L2-11catwk","slide":42,"slides":[42],"q":"Why can polar substances not simply pass through the cell membrane?","o":["The lipid bilayer has high electrical resistance","The membrane is made mainly of water-filled channels","Membrane enzymes destroy every polar molecule on contact","The bilayer is rigid and contains no phospholipid molecules"],"a":0,"e":"The lipid molecules give the membrane fluidity and high electrical resistance, so polar substances cannot penetrate."},{"id":"L2-1f6z5zv","slide":43,"slides":[43],"q":"What role do proteins embedded in the cell membrane play in pharmacology?","o":["They make the membrane completely impermeable to all drugs","They store drugs inside the cell as long-term reservoirs","They convert drugs into polar metabolites for excretion","They act as receptors, selective targets for drugs"],"a":3,"e":"Embedded proteins serve as receptors that start signalling pathways inside the cell."},{"id":"L2-1wbsjar","slide":44,"slides":[44],"q":"Which mechanism is the most important for drug transport across membranes?","o":["Endocytosis","Active transport","Carrier-linked exchange","Passive diffusion"],"a":3,"e":"Passive diffusion is stressed as the most important mechanism."},{"id":"L2-1sua876","slide":45,"slides":[45],"q":"Which substances cross membranes by passive diffusion, as in the transport table?","o":["Ionised weak acids moved by energy-driven carriers","Large molecules engulfed in membrane vesicles","Non-electrolytes and unionised weak acids and bases","Water and solutes carried through filtration pores only"],"a":2,"e":"Passive diffusion suits non-electrolytes and the unionised form of weak acids and bases."},{"id":"L2-j5n5li","slide":46,"slides":[46],"q":"Passive diffusion moves drug molecules:","o":["Against a gradient using external cellular energy","Inside droplets engulfed by the cell membrane","Down a concentration gradient across a membrane","Along a pressure gradient through aqueous pores"],"a":2,"e":"Passive diffusion is passage down a concentration gradient."},{"id":"L2-8g16f0","slide":47,"slides":[47],"q":"Bulk flow is best described as:","o":["Fluid and solutes moving along a pressure gradient through pores","Movement of drug down a gradient by dissolving in the membrane lipid","Movement of drug against a gradient using membrane carrier proteins","Movement of drug in vesicles pinched off from the membrane surface"],"a":0,"e":"Bulk flow is the total movement of fluid and solutes along a pressure gradient through intercellular pores."},{"id":"L2-1wu7nv7","slide":48,"slides":[48],"q":"Carrier-mediated transport comes in which two types?","o":["Passive diffusion and bulk transport","Lipid and aqueous pore transport","Filtration and vesicular endocytosis","Active and facilitated transport"],"a":3,"e":"Both types need carrier molecules in the membrane: active and facilitated."},{"id":"L2-lzu6ui","slide":49,"slides":[49],"q":"How does endocytosis move a drug across a membrane?","o":["It dissolves in the lipid layer and diffuses down a gradient","It is carried across by a membrane protein using external energy","It is engulfed in droplets then released on the other side","It flows through intercellular pores along a pressure gradient"],"a":2,"e":"Drugs are engulfed in small droplets at one side and released at the other."},{"id":"L2-1ov8jhy","slide":50,"slides":[50],"q":"According to Fick's law, which change would SLOW the rate of absorption?","o":["A higher dose","A larger surface area","A thicker membrane","Greater lipid solubility"],"a":2,"e":"Rate rises with dose, lipid solubility, temperature and surface area, and falls with molecular size and membrane thickness."},{"id":"L2-10rmkzl","slide":50,"slides":[50],"q":"Fick's law divides the rate by the square root of molecular size. What does this predict?","o":["Bigger molecules are absorbed more quickly","Bigger molecules are absorbed more slowly","Molecular size has no effect on the rate","Only polar molecules are affected by size"],"a":1,"e":"Larger molecular size sits in the denominator, so absorption is slower."},{"id":"L2-lrrata","slide":51,"slides":[51],"q":"As polarity of a molecule increases, its lipid solubility:","o":["Rises","Stays the same","Falls","Doubles"],"a":2,"e":"Higher polarity means lower lipid solubility."},{"id":"L2-1k6tymm","slide":51,"slides":[51],"q":"Which group of molecules includes the bulk of drugs?","o":["Non-polar, lipid-soluble non-electrolytes","Polar, water-soluble non-electrolytes","Weak electrolytes (acids or bases)","Strong electrolytes that ionise fully"],"a":2,"e":"Most drugs are weak electrolytes, the third group based on polarity."},{"id":"L2-1p1nd26","slide":52,"slides":[52],"q":"The three sub-mechanisms of passive diffusion are:","o":["Active, facilitated and lipid diffusion","Bulk, aqueous and ionic diffusion","Lipid, aqueous and non-ionic diffusion","Lipid, pore and carrier diffusion"],"a":2,"e":"Based on polarity, drugs cross by lipid, aqueous or non-ionic diffusion."},{"id":"L2-1ci0lv9","slide":53,"slides":[53],"q":"Which drugs use lipid diffusion most effectively?","o":["Polar, water-soluble substances","Non-polar, lipid-soluble substances","Ionised weak electrolytes","Molecules with high molecular weight"],"a":1,"e":"Lipid diffusion is limited by lipid solubility and used best by non-polar substances."},{"id":"L2-e9kqlu","slide":54,"slides":[54],"q":"Polar drugs cross barriers by aqueous diffusion through:","o":["The lipid core of the cell membrane bilayer itself","Carrier proteins in the membrane that use cellular energy","Water-filled pores in the intercellular cement","Droplets formed by invagination of the cell membrane"],"a":2,"e":"Polar drugs pass through water-filled pores between cells (paracellular passage or filtration)."},{"id":"L2-v7twb4","slide":54,"slides":[54],"q":"What limits aqueous diffusion of a polar drug?","o":["Its molecular size and the pore diameter","Its lipid solubility and the thickness of the membrane","Its pKa and the pH of the surrounding tissue fluid","The supply of cellular energy available to the carriers"],"a":0,"e":"Aqueous diffusion is limited by molecular size and the diameter of the pores."},{"id":"L2-e7ix1x","slide":55,"slides":[55],"q":"Which form of a weak electrolyte crosses the membrane by non-ionic diffusion?","o":["The fully ionised moiety","The non-ionised moiety","Both forms to an equal degree","Neither form can cross at all"],"a":1,"e":"Only the non-ionised moiety diffuses across the biological membrane."},{"id":"L2-g5urom","slide":56,"slides":[56],"q":"A drug must be lipid-soluble to cross the membrane, but it must also:","o":["Be completely ionised so that it dissolves readily in plasma","Be bound to albumin before it can reach the membrane surface","Be in aqueous solution (plasma) to reach the membrane","Be insoluble in water so that it remains within the lipid"],"a":2,"e":"Drugs dissolve in the membrane lipids to cross it, yet must be in aqueous solution to reach it."},{"id":"L2-1mcanbt","slide":57,"slides":[57],"q":"Which two factors decide the passage of weak electrolytes through lipid barriers?","o":["Molecular weight of the drug and the dose given","pKa of the drug and pH of its surroundings","Blood flow and the absorbing surface area","Lipid solubility and the temperature"],"a":1,"e":"The pKa of the electrolyte and the pH of the medium, as shown by Henderson-Hasselbalch."},{"id":"L2-ybnngk","slide":59,"slides":[59],"q":"For a weak acid, which form is the ionised one?","o":["The unprotonated form","The protonated form","Both forms are ionised","Neither form is ionised"],"a":0,"e":"For a weak acid, protonated means nonionised and unprotonated means ionised."},{"id":"L2-dxsptq","slide":60,"slides":[60],"q":"For a weak base, which form is the ionised one?","o":["The unprotonated form","Both forms are ionised","The protonated form","Neither form is ionised"],"a":2,"e":"For a weak base, protonated means ionised and unprotonated means nonionised."},{"id":"L2-1llq6t8","slide":58,"slides":[58],"q":"A weak acid is in a medium whose pH is well below its pKa. Which form predominates?","o":["Mostly non-ionised, so it crosses membranes easily","Mostly ionised, so it crosses membranes very easily","Mostly ionised, so it becomes trapped in the lipid bilayer","Equal parts of each form regardless of the medium's pH"],"a":0,"e":"pKa minus pH is positive, so the non-ionised (protonated) form predominates for a weak acid."},{"id":"L2-27z84w","slide":61,"slides":[61],"q":"Why can the absorption of the same drug differ in different parts of the GIT?","o":["pH differs between areas, altering ionisation","The GIT has no pH difference but differs in colour","Every part of the GIT has the same surface area","Drugs only cross membranes in the stomach and colon"],"a":0,"e":"pH differs within one body segment, so ionisation and absorption rate change."},{"id":"L2-1npvzlp","slide":62,"slides":[62],"q":"Absorption of tetracycline in the GIT is greater in which region?","o":["The lower segments, falling in the upper parts","The upper segments, falling in the lower parts","The rectum only, whatever the pH","Equal in every segment of the GIT"],"a":1,"e":"Tetracycline absorption is greater in the upper segments and lower in the lower parts."},{"id":"L2-1ta8vjg","slide":62,"slides":[62],"q":"In poisoning, why might urine be alkalinised or acidified?","o":["To change ionisation so more drug is excreted","To stop the kidney from filtering the poison","To raise plasma protein binding of the poison","To increase tubular reabsorption of the drug"],"a":0,"e":"Altering urine pH changes ionisation and so facilitates excretion of drugs or poisons."},{"id":"L2-1tewdjq","slide":63,"slides":[63],"q":"Why is changing urine pH of little use for eliminating penicillins?","o":["They are never filtered by the kidney at all","They are completely metabolised before reaching the kidney","They leave mainly by tubular secretion (about 90%)","They are ionised at every urine pH value"],"a":2,"e":"Renal elimination of penicillins is basically tubular secretion and not subject to pH changes."},{"id":"L2-10jtuv5","slide":64,"slides":[64],"q":"In liver failure, which gut-derived substance enters the circulation and causes hepatic encephalopathy?","o":["Lactic acid from the intestinal lining","Ammonia (NH3) from intestinal flora","Bilirubin from red cell breakdown","Urea from dietary protein"],"a":1,"e":"Metabolic activity of intestinal flora raises circulating NH3, causing portal-systemic encephalopathy."},{"id":"L2-t8brem","slide":65,"slides":[65],"q":"How does lactulose help reduce blood ammonia?","o":["It makes the gut alkaline so NH3 is absorbed faster","It blocks bacteria from entering the portal vein","It acidifies the gut so NH3 is trapped as NH4+","It increases the hepatic breakdown of bilirubin"],"a":2,"e":"Lactulose gives lactic acid, H+ combines with NH3 to NH4+ (ion trapping), and NH4+ is excreted."},{"id":"L2-pya1xq","slide":66,"slides":[66],"q":"Bulk flow is able to move drug across capillary walls because:","o":["Capillary walls are lined by a thick continuous lipid layer","Capillary walls exclude every water-soluble substance entirely","Substances cross the capillary wall far faster than membranes","Capillary walls use cellular energy to actively pump all drugs"],"a":2,"e":"Substances, lipid or not, cross the capillary wall at rates much greater than across membranes."},{"id":"L2-1cuu4tl","slide":67,"slides":[67],"q":"Bulk flow is the most important way capillaries carry drug to tissues after which routes?","o":["Oral and rectal administration of tablets","Subcutaneous and intramuscular injection","Sublingual and buccal mucosal administration","Inhalation and topical application to skin"],"a":1,"e":"Bulk flow is the key mechanism after parenteral administration, especially SC and IM."},{"id":"L2-vt92oc","slide":67,"slides":[67],"q":"Why is bulk flow absent in the capillaries of the CNS?","o":["The blood-brain barrier is present","The brain has no blood supply","CNS capillaries are too large","CNS capillaries are lined with lipids only"],"a":0,"e":"Bulk flow is absent in CNS capillaries because of the BBB."},{"id":"L2-1cvvxwt","slide":68,"slides":[68],"q":"Which set lists the factors that influence bulk flow?","o":["Lipid solubility, pKa, temperature and membrane thickness","Blood flow, interstitial solubility and capillary surface area","Carrier number, energy supply and saturation kinetics of transport","Molecular weight, dose, membrane thickness and lipid solubility"],"a":1,"e":"Blood flow, drug solubility in the interstitium and capillary surface area determine bulk flow."},{"id":"L2-1c2jywl","slide":69,"slides":[69],"q":"Carrier-mediated mechanisms need what in the cell membrane?","o":["Aqueous pores","Carrier molecules","A lipid gradient","Pressure gradient"],"a":1,"e":"Active and facilitated transport both depend on carrier molecules."},{"id":"L2-zofaj9","slide":70,"slides":[70],"q":"Two drugs compete for the same carrier and one lowers transport of the other. Which feature of active transport is this?","o":["Saturable carrier kinetics","Competitive inhibition","Carrier stereospecificity","Bulk flow through pores"],"a":1,"e":"Competitive inhibition is among the features of active transport."},{"id":"L2-ldg2lw","slide":70,"slides":[70],"q":"Which feature separates active transport from passive diffusion?","o":["It moves drug against the gradient using energy","It moves molecules only down the concentration gradient","It needs no carrier proteins in the membrane at all","It shows no saturation even at high concentrations"],"a":0,"e":"Active transport needs external energy and moves molecules against the gradient."},{"id":"L2-1rm3twu","slide":71,"slides":[71],"q":"Active transport occurs in the membranes of which structures?","o":["Red blood cells, hair follicles, sweat ducts and fat cells","Cartilage, tendons, bone, fat cells and skeletal ligaments","Renal tubules, choroid plexus, neurons, hepatocytes","Corneal cells, nails, hair shafts and the stratum corneum"],"a":2,"e":"Active transport occurs in renal tubules, choroid plexus, neuronal membranes and hepatocytes."},{"id":"L2-168qw7z","slide":72,"slides":[72],"q":"How does facilitated transport differ from active transport?","o":["It does not use carrier proteins at all","It moves drugs only against a concentration gradient","It is never saturable and never competitive","It needs no external energy, though it uses carriers"],"a":3,"e":"Facilitated transport has all the features of active transport except the need for energy."},{"id":"L2-1nemw19","slide":73,"slides":[73],"q":"Which drugs rely on endocytosis, and how important is it?","o":["Small polar molecules; the main route for most drugs","Large molecules such as vaccines; of limited importance","Lipid-soluble drugs of low weight; the most important route","Ionised weak acids; the main route in the renal tubule"],"a":1,"e":"Endocytosis suits drugs of molecular weight above 1000, such as vaccines, and is of limited importance."},{"id":"L2-5f4xba","slide":74,"slides":[74],"q":"Which of these is NOT listed as a factor influencing drug absorption?","o":["Blood supply at the absorbing site","Formulation of the drug","Degree of plasma protein binding","Route of administration"],"a":2,"e":"The listed factors are dose, physicochemical characteristics, blood supply, surface area, formulation and route."},{"id":"L2-180zxei","slide":75,"slides":[75],"q":"Why are liquid drugs usually absorbed faster than solid ones?","o":["Liquids are always more lipid-soluble than solids","Solids are always larger molecules than liquids","Solids must first be liberated into solution","Liquids bypass the absorbing membrane entirely"],"a":2,"e":"Solid forms need to be liberated and go into solution before they can be absorbed."},{"id":"L2-1oc0i11","slide":76,"slides":[76],"q":"Which physicochemical properties influence drug absorption?","o":["Colour, taste, shape and brand name of the tablet","Lipid solubility, pKa and local pH, molecular size","Plasma half-life, clearance and volume of distribution","Dose, route of administration, formulation and patient age"],"a":1,"e":"The three listed are lipid solubility, pKa/pH and molecular size."},{"id":"L2-pw46vl","slide":77,"slides":[77],"q":"A drug has a LOW lipid:water partition coefficient. What happens to its absorption?","o":["It crosses membranes faster than lipid-soluble drugs","It stays in the aqueous phase and is poorly absorbed","It is absorbed only by endocytosis","It is absorbed fully whatever the route"],"a":1,"e":"A low coefficient means low lipid solubility, so the drug remains in the aqueous phase and absorption falls."},{"id":"L2-1b29vyi","slide":78,"slides":[78],"q":"Why do oil-based penicillin depot injections need to be given less often?","o":["The oil converts the penicillin into a prodrug","The oil binds penicillin permanently to albumin","A high lipid:water coefficient releases the drug slowly","The oil makes the penicillin pass through the kidney faster"],"a":2,"e":"Depot formulations exploit a high partition coefficient so the drug is released slowly."},{"id":"L2-1vyzqdg","slide":79,"slides":[79],"q":"At a site where a weak acid is mostly ionised, what happens to its absorption?","o":["It falls, since ionised forms cannot cross the lipid","It rises, since ionised forms are highly lipid-soluble","It is unchanged, since ionisation is not relevant here","It is blocked only when a very large dose is given"],"a":0,"e":"Ionised forms do not permeate the lipid layer; non-ionised forms pass through and are absorbed."},{"id":"L2-1xf0vmq","slide":80,"slides":[80],"q":"How does molecular size affect absorption?","o":["Larger molecules are absorbed more rapidly and completely","Size affects only aqueous solubility and not absorption rate","Smaller molecules are absorbed more rapidly and completely","Size matters only for drugs given by injection or infusion"],"a":2,"e":"Smaller molecular size gives more rapid and complete absorption."},{"id":"L2-mrqjas","slide":81,"slides":[81],"q":"Why does a larger dose tend to speed absorption?","o":["It raises the concentration gradient","It lowers the drug's lipid solubility at the site","It makes the absorbing membrane physically thinner","It reduces the drug's molecular size at the site"],"a":0,"e":"A higher dose raises drug concentration at the site, increasing the gradient and the rate of absorption."},{"id":"L2-1qvg2mg","slide":82,"slides":[82],"q":"Which two sites offer large absorbing surface areas?","o":["Alveolar epithelium and intestinal mucosa","Buccal mucosa and the sublingual mucosal surface","Gastric mucosa and the conjunctival epithelium","Nasal mucosa and the urethral mucosal lining"],"a":0,"e":"The lecture gives the alveolar epithelium and intestinal mucosa as large absorbing surfaces."},{"id":"L2-xyonp6","slide":83,"slides":[83],"q":"A local anaesthetic is combined with a vasoconstrictor. What is the effect?","o":["Absorption rises and the local effect is shorter","Absorption is unchanged but the dose must double","Absorption falls and the effect lasts longer","The anaesthetic is converted to an active prodrug"],"a":2,"e":"Vasoconstrictors reduce blood flow, so absorption falls and the therapeutic effect is prolonged."},{"id":"L2-wki8zq","slide":83,"slides":[83],"q":"Which action would speed absorption of an injected drug?","o":["Applying a vasoconstrictor at the site","Applying ice to the injection site","Massage or local warmth at the site","Giving the drug in an oily vehicle"],"a":2,"e":"Massage and heat increase blood flow to the site and so increase absorption."},{"id":"L2-1cpzrld","slide":84,"slides":[84],"q":"Which of these is a solid dosage form that must dissolve before it is absorbed?","o":["Syrup","Pessary","Suspension","Lotion"],"a":1,"e":"Tablets, capsules, caplets, pills, suppositories and pessaries are solid forms; syrups, suspensions and lotions are liquid or semisolid."}]},{"id":"L3","title":"Enteral Routes","sub":"Oral, tubes, sublingual, buccal and rectal administration","emoji":"💧","slides":[85,110],"recap":["Enteral routes put the drug directly into the GIT (oral, NG/OG/G/J tubes, buccal, sublingual, rectal); parenteral routes bypass it. The route decides the surface area and how much drug reaches the circulation.","Oral is safe, cheap and convenient, but absorption is irregular, unsuitable in vomiting or unconscious patients, and subject to first-pass loss, acid, enzymes and enterohepatic recycling.","Sublingual drugs drain directly into the superior vena cava and skip first-pass: ideal for potent, lipid-soluble emergency drugs such as nitroglycerin.","Rectal is useful when oral is not possible, and about half the drug avoids first-pass, but absorption is irregular and incomplete."],"questions":[{"id":"L3-1e70yt7","slide":85,"slides":[85],"q":"The word 'parenteral' in the routes-of-administration chart literally means:","o":["Within the intestine, reaching the portal vein","Inside the stomach and small bowel only","Under the tongue or inside the cheek","Beyond the intestine, bypassing the GIT"],"a":3,"e":"Enteral routes go through the intestine; parenteral routes go beyond it."},{"id":"L3-17lv01z","slide":86,"slides":[86],"q":"Why does the route of administration matter for absorption?","o":["It decides the colour and taste of the formulation","It changes the drug's chemical name","It fixes the drug's half-life for all patients","It sets the surface area available for absorption"],"a":3,"e":"Routes determine the absorbing surface area and so the extent of absorption into circulation."},{"id":"L3-1s1jxqf","slide":87,"slides":[87],"q":"Enteral administration delivers a drug:","o":["Into the GIT by way of a needle","Past the GIT into the bloodstream","Through the skin into the dermis","Directly into the GIT"],"a":3,"e":"Enteral means directly into the GIT; parenteral means bypassing the GIT."},{"id":"L3-xggqmg","slide":88,"slides":[88],"q":"Which of these is an enteral route?","o":["Nasogastric tube","Intravenous injection","Intramuscular injection","Intrathecal injection"],"a":0,"e":"NG, OG, G and J tubes, oral, buccal and sublingual are enteral routes."},{"id":"L3-1as7dfb","slide":88,"slides":[88],"q":"Which tube delivers drug or feed directly into the jejunum?","o":["G-tube","NG-tube","OG-tube","J-tube"],"a":3,"e":"The J-tube goes to the jejunum; the G-tube goes to the stomach."},{"id":"L3-whl0ie","slide":89,"slides":[89,90,91],"q":"A nasogastric tube reaches the stomach by passing through the nose and which structure?","o":["The upper trachea","The duodenal bulb","The oesophagus","The laryngeal inlet"],"a":2,"e":"The diagram labels the nasogastric tube passing down the oesophagus to the stomach, with a funnel for administration."},{"id":"L3-1akbldx","slide":95,"slides":[95],"q":"A tablet must be liberated before it touches the absorbing surface. This belongs to which factor group for oral bioavailability?","o":["Physiological variables","Dosage form","Interactions with GIT components","Physicochemical properties"],"a":1,"e":"Dosage form: the drug has to be liberated before making contact with the absorbing surface."},{"id":"L3-3g2451","slide":95,"slides":[95],"q":"Which pair of physicochemical properties influences oral bioavailability?","o":["Gastric emptying time and peristalsis","Lipid solubility and ionisation","Food and pH interactions in the gut","Dosage form and absorbing surface area"],"a":1,"e":"The physicochemical factors listed are lipid solubility and degree of ionisation."},{"id":"L3-19djnpr","slide":96,"slides":[96],"q":"Which is a physiological variable affecting oral absorption?","o":["Lipid solubility of the drug","The tablet formulation","Food-drug interactions","Gastric emptying time"],"a":3,"e":"Physiological variables include surface area, gastric emptying time, motility and presystemic loss."},{"id":"L3-qj81c9","slide":96,"slides":[96],"q":"Presystemic loss (the first-pass effect) in the gut involves which structures?","o":["Gastric emptying and intestinal motility","Intestinal bacteria and mucosal enzymes","Bile salts and gallbladder storage cycles","Plasma proteins and circulating red cells"],"a":1,"e":"Presystemic loss is due to bacteria in the lumen and enzymes of the intestinal epithelium."},{"id":"L3-b7zte7","slide":97,"slides":[97],"q":"Which GIT interactions can change oral absorption?","o":["Lipid solubility and degree of ionisation","Dosage form and gastric emptying time","Absorbing surface area and blood flow","pH effects and food-drug interactions"],"a":3,"e":"Interactions with GIT components include pH effects, food and other drugs."},{"id":"L3-wxozsg","slide":98,"slides":[98],"q":"Why is the oral route called safe in overdose?","o":["The drug can still be retrieved if overdosed","The drug is destroyed before it reaches the stomach","The drug cannot be absorbed in large amounts","The drug is always sterile and therefore harmless"],"a":0,"e":"Safe, enabling the drug to be retrieved in case of overdose."},{"id":"L3-1nf8b5k","slide":98,"slides":[98],"q":"Why does the oral route encourage self-medication?","o":["No special equipment or expertise is needed","It is the only route that avoids all drug losses","It always gives the fastest possible onset","It guarantees the exact bioavailability"],"a":0,"e":"It is very cheap and requires no special equipment or expertise, and the drug need not be sterile."},{"id":"L3-156q4oj","slide":99,"slides":[99],"q":"Which feature of the GIT mixes drug with its contents well?","o":["Gastric acid","Bile secretion","Portal blood flow","Peristalsis"],"a":3,"e":"Good mixing of contents is due to peristalsis, along with the large absorbing surface."},{"id":"L3-5r1rcc","slide":100,"slides":[100],"q":"Which patients should NOT receive drugs by the oral route?","o":["Those who are vomiting or unconscious","Those who are fully awake and able to swallow","Those who need a very large absorbing surface","Those who self-medicate at home"],"a":0,"e":"The oral route is contraindicated for vomiting and unconscious individuals."},{"id":"L3-1vdxijz","slide":100,"slides":[100],"q":"Why is the oral bioavailability of a drug hard to predict?","o":["The drug is always destroyed completely","The stomach has no absorbing surface","Oral drugs must always be sterile","Absorption by this route is irregular"],"a":3,"e":"Extent of absorption cannot be predicted because oral absorption is irregular."},{"id":"L3-jqfwyi","slide":101,"slides":[101],"q":"Why is insulin not given by mouth?","o":["Gastric acid converts it to a prodrug","It is too lipid-soluble to dissolve","Digestive enzymes destroy peptide drugs","It is excreted unchanged in the stomach"],"a":2,"e":"Peptide drugs such as insulin are destroyed by digestive enzymes."},{"id":"L3-190pe4r","slide":101,"slides":[101],"q":"Which agent can destroy some penicillins given by mouth?","o":["Bile salts","Plasma proteins","Sweat enzymes","Gastric acid"],"a":3,"e":"Some penicillins are destroyed by gastric acid; others by enzymes, flora and hepatic enzymes."},{"id":"L3-c4k5tk","slide":102,"slides":[102],"q":"What is meant by the first-pass effect of an oral drug?","o":["Loss of drug in gut mucosa and liver before circulation","Immediate absorption of the drug through the stomach lining","Binding of the drug to plasma albumin after absorption","Elimination of the drug by the kidney within the first hour"],"a":0,"e":"Gut mucosa plus liver cause presystemic loss and so lower bioavailability."},{"id":"L3-svscdd","slide":102,"slides":[102],"q":"Which sequence describes enterohepatic recycling?","o":["Stomach, kidney, bladder, urine, back to the stomach","Intestine, portal blood, hepatocytes, bile, intestine","Skin, dermis, capillaries, lymph nodes, back to the skin","Lungs, alveoli, pulmonary blood, bile, back to the lungs"],"a":1,"e":"Drug passes from intestine to portal blood to hepatocytes to bile and back into the intestine, as with digoxin."},{"id":"L3-1otknze","slide":103,"slides":[103],"q":"In the enterohepatic diagram, where is bile stored between meals?","o":["Portal vein","Hepatic sinusoids","Gallbladder","Colon"],"a":2,"e":"The diagram shows storage in the gallbladder with postprandial secretion into the intestine."},{"id":"L3-1qjnhu2","slide":103,"slides":[103],"q":"In the enterohepatic diagram, how is drug and bile reabsorbed in the ileum and colon?","o":["Passive transport in the ileum, active transport in the colon","Active transport in both the ileum and the colon","Active transport in the ileum, passive transport in the colon","Endocytosis in the ileum, bulk flow in the colon"],"a":2,"e":"The diagram labels ileal active transport and colonic passive transport."},{"id":"L3-hzk4cg","slide":104,"slides":[104],"q":"Why can enterohepatic recycling be good or bad for a patient?","o":["It depends on the drug's cumulative effect","It always makes a drug safer and more effective","It always lowers the oral bioavailability of the drug","It occurs only with drugs that are injected parenterally"],"a":0,"e":"Recycling may be advantageous or disadvantageous depending on the cumulative effect of the drug."},{"id":"L3-6ym1q5","slide":104,"slides":[104],"q":"Compared with injection, how quickly does an oral drug usually reach its therapeutic effect?","o":["Instantly","Relatively slowly","At the same speed","Faster, because of first-pass"],"a":1,"e":"A disadvantage of the oral route is that it takes relatively longer to achieve a therapeutic effect."},{"id":"L3-1nhomfe","slide":105,"slides":[105],"q":"Why do sublingual drugs avoid hepatic first-pass metabolism?","o":["Venous drainage goes through the portal vein to the liver","The drug is absorbed only in the stomach and duodenal bulb","Veins drain into the superior vena cava, not the liver","The drug is destroyed by salivary enzymes before absorption"],"a":2,"e":"Sublingual venous drainage reaches the general circulation directly via the superior vena cava."},{"id":"L3-1wmbk4i","slide":105,"slides":[105],"q":"Which describes the oral mucosa used for sublingual dosing?","o":["Large surface area but poorly vascularised","Large surface area and poorly vascularised","Small surface area but highly vascularised","Small surface area and poorly vascularised"],"a":2,"e":"The oral mucosae have a small surface area but are highly vascularised."},{"id":"L3-1t6rutq","slide":106,"slides":[106],"q":"By which two pathways can a drug cross the buccal or sublingual mucosa, as drawn?","o":["Active and facilitated transport","Bulk flow and vesicular endocytosis","Transcellular and paracellular","Enteral and parenteral diffusion"],"a":2,"e":"The diagram shows transcellular and paracellular passage across the mucosal membrane to blood vessels."},{"id":"L3-1r6sqtl","slide":107,"slides":[107],"q":"Which drug is the classic sublingual emergency treatment for angina pectoris?","o":["Nifedipine capsule","Nitroglycerin","Digoxin","Insulin"],"a":1,"e":"Sublingual route suits very potent, highly lipid-soluble emergency drugs such as nitroglycerin."},{"id":"L3-lhn54x","slide":107,"slides":[107],"q":"What does the lecture say about nifedipine powder given sublingually to lower raised BP rapidly?","o":["It is the recommended first choice","The practice has been discontinued","It is limited to children","It is given only by injection"],"a":1,"e":"Sublingual nifedipine for rapid BP reduction is a discontinued practice."},{"id":"L3-8fct8z","slide":108,"slides":[108,109],"q":"Why does a rectal drug only partly escape first-pass metabolism?","o":["All rectal veins drain straight into the hepatic portal vein","No rectal veins drain into the inferior vena cava at all","The rectal wall has too little blood supply to absorb","Only about half the venous drainage enters the portal vein"],"a":3,"e":"About 50% drains via the internal haemorrhoidal vein into the portal circulation; the rest via the external vein to the inferior vena cava."},{"id":"L3-1ewot9r","slide":108,"slides":[108],"q":"Which patient is a good candidate for the rectal route?","o":["A fully conscious adult who is swallowing normally","A patient needing an instant intravenous effect","A patient with severe rectal irritation","A convulsing or unconscious child"],"a":3,"e":"The rectal route is used when oral administration is not feasible, especially in children and convulsing or unconscious patients."},{"id":"L3-xplzb","slide":110,"slides":[110],"q":"Which problems limit the rectal route?","o":["Complete first-pass loss and a very rapid onset of effect","Need for sterile equipment and specially trained staff","Destruction by gastric acid and intestinal digestive enzymes","Irregular, incomplete absorption and mucosal irritation"],"a":3,"e":"The rectal route gives irregular and incomplete absorption; irritation of mucosa can cause ejection of the drug."}]},{"id":"L4","title":"Parenteral Routes","sub":"Injections, topical, transdermal and inhalation routes","emoji":"💉","slides":[111,149],"recap":["Parenteral routes bypass the GIT. Injection routes are invasive (IV, IM, SC, intrathecal, epidural, intra-arterial and others); topical, transdermal and inhalational routes are non-invasive.","IV gives complete bioavailability, rapid onset and predictable levels, but cannot be recalled, needs sterility and skill, and risks embolus, septicaemia and anaphylaxis.","IM absorption depends on the muscle and blood flow (deltoid is faster than gluteus; exercise speeds it); SC is slower and suits slow, continuous release.","Intrathecal bypasses the BBB, epidural helps in obstetrics, intraosseous is for collapsed veins; topical drugs can still be absorbed systemically (timolol drops and asthma)."],"questions":[{"id":"L4-1unicrc","slide":111,"slides":[111],"q":"Which statement defines parenteral routes?","o":["The GIT is bypassed","The drug is swallowed","The drug passes through the stomach","The drug is absorbed by the intestine"],"a":0,"e":"Parenteral routes bypass the GIT and are split into injection and non-injection routes."},{"id":"L4-1skjl4u","slide":111,"slides":[111],"q":"Which group of parenteral routes is invasive?","o":["Topical routes","Transdermal routes","Injection routes","Inhalational routes"],"a":2,"e":"Injection routes are invasive; topical, transdermal and inhalational routes are non-invasive."},{"id":"L4-juvsqv","slide":112,"slides":[112],"q":"Which of these is NOT a parenteral injection route?","o":["Intrathecal","Epidural","Intraosseous","Sublingual"],"a":3,"e":"Sublingual is an enteral route; the others are listed injection routes."},{"id":"L4-8ojdfz","slide":113,"slides":[113],"q":"Why can an irritating drug be given intravenously?","o":["Veins lack endothelium, so there is nothing to irritate","The liver removes the irritant before it reaches tissues","The drug is converted to a prodrug once in the blood","Blood dilutes it and blood vessels are insensitive"],"a":3,"e":"Irritating drugs can be given IV since blood dilutes them and blood vessels are insensitive."},{"id":"L4-1v0j8rp","slide":113,"slides":[113],"q":"Why is IV bioavailability complete?","o":["The drug is absorbed rapidly through the stomach lining","Absorption is bypassed; the drug enters blood directly","The liver converts every dose to an active form first","The kidney prevents any of the drug being excreted"],"a":1,"e":"IV administration bypasses absorption and first-pass, so complete bioavailability is assured."},{"id":"L4-j43i1s","slide":114,"slides":[114],"q":"Why must the IV dose be computed precisely?","o":["Once injected, the drug cannot be taken out","The drug is slowly absorbed and cannot accumulate","Veins can store extra drug safely for later","The drug is destroyed by gastric acid"],"a":0,"e":"Once the drug is injected you cannot get it out, so errors risk toxicity."},{"id":"L4-2j4u9p","slide":115,"slides":[115],"q":"Which formulation is unsuitable for intravenous injection?","o":["A drug dissolved in sterile saline","A drug in an oily vehicle","A drug in a clear aqueous solution","A drug in a dilute sterile infusion"],"a":1,"e":"IV is not suitable for drugs with oily vehicles or drugs that lyse red blood cells."},{"id":"L4-s6yc03","slide":115,"slides":[115],"q":"Which pair lists two complications of intravenous injection?","o":["First-pass loss and gastric irritation","Slow onset and incomplete bioavailability","Enterohepatic recycling and irregular absorption","Embolus and septicaemia"],"a":3,"e":"IV risks include embolus (air or clot), septicaemia from contamination and anaphylaxis."},{"id":"L4-1lx9nx","slide":116,"slides":[116],"q":"Which muscle is NOT named as an IM injection site in the lecture?","o":["Vastus lateralis","Biceps brachii","Rectus femoris","Gluteus maximus"],"a":1,"e":"The IM sites named are deltoid, vastus lateralis, rectus femoris and gluteus maximus."},{"id":"L4-1bells8","slide":117,"slides":[117],"q":"Which site tends to absorb an IM drug faster?","o":["The deltoid, compared with the gluteus maximus","The gluteus maximus, compared with the deltoid","Both sites absorb at exactly the same rate","The gluteus maximus in obese patients"],"a":0,"e":"Absorption from the deltoid is greater than from the gluteus maximus."},{"id":"L4-1bzg45s","slide":117,"slides":[117],"q":"Why is IM absorption slower in obese individuals?","o":["Fat is poorly perfused, so absorption falls","Fat tissue destroys the drug before absorption","Fat has a larger surface area for absorption","Fat tissue speeds the drug into the portal vein"],"a":0,"e":"Obese individuals show reduced IM absorption because gluteal fat has less blood flow."},{"id":"L4-15libzk","slide":118,"slides":[118],"q":"A patient with insulin-dependent diabetes injects insulin into the thigh and then starts running. What is the risk?","o":["Hypoglycaemia from faster absorption during exercise","Hyperglycaemia from slower absorption during exercise","No change, since exercise never affects absorption","Anaphylaxis from the muscle contraction"],"a":0,"e":"Exercise raises muscle blood flow and absorption, risking hypoglycaemia in insulin-dependent diabetics."},{"id":"L4-glpaoa","slide":119,"slides":[119,120],"q":"Which nerves are labelled near the deltoid and the gluteal injection sites, in that order?","o":["Sciatic nerve, then radial nerve","Ulnar nerve, then femoral nerve","Radial nerve, then sciatic nerve","Radial nerve, then femoral nerve"],"a":2,"e":"The deltoid diagram shows the radial nerve and the gluteal diagram shows the sciatic nerve."},{"id":"L4-11dk8s5","slide":121,"slides":[121],"q":"The injection-site diagram using the anterior superior iliac spine and greater trochanter as landmarks is for which area?","o":["The upper arm (deltoid region)","The hip (gluteal) region","The mid-thigh (vastus region)","The lower back (lumbar region)"],"a":1,"e":"The landmarks locate the gluteal injection area."},{"id":"L4-v42vzn","slide":122,"slides":[122],"q":"Vastus lateralis injections are given in which part of the thigh?","o":["The inner upper third","The back of the lower third","The front of the upper third","The outer middle third"],"a":3,"e":"The diagram marks the outer middle third of the vastus lateralis."},{"id":"L4-12an8ys","slide":124,"slides":[124],"q":"Why is subcutaneous absorption slower than IM absorption?","o":["Blood flow to these areas is lower","Subcutaneous tissue has no capillaries","The drug is destroyed in fat","Subcutaneous drugs cross the BBB"],"a":0,"e":"Blood flow to subcutaneous areas is low, so absorption is slower than IM."},{"id":"L4-j5i3t4","slide":124,"slides":[124],"q":"Why are vasoconstrictors sometimes added to SC drugs?","o":["To slow absorption even further","To speed up absorption","To stop the drug being absorbed at all","To make the drug lipid-soluble"],"a":0,"e":"Vasoconstrictors reduce blood flow and slow absorption further."},{"id":"L4-1p0welp","slide":125,"slides":[125],"q":"Which dosage forms are used SC for slow release?","o":["Syrups and bitters","Pellets and discs","Pessaries and suppositories","Gels and creams"],"a":1,"e":"SC drugs of high lipid solubility are given as pellets, discs and so on for slow release."},{"id":"L4-1vpt8vh","slide":125,"slides":[125],"q":"What is the danger of giving an irritant drug subcutaneously?","o":["Immediate bradycardia","Necrosis of the tissue","Complete loss of drug in the liver","Rapid excretion in the urine"],"a":1,"e":"An irritant given SC may cause necrosis of the surrounding tissue."},{"id":"L4-15om6hi","slide":126,"slides":[126],"q":"Why is the intrathecal route used?","o":["To avoid first-pass metabolism after oral dosing","To give very large fluid volumes safely over time","To bypass the BBB for a rapid local effect","To slow the absorption of a drug from muscle"],"a":2,"e":"The BBB keeps many drugs out; injection into the subarachnoid space gives a rapid local effect."},{"id":"L4-jjqyab","slide":127,"slides":[127],"q":"Where does an intrathecal injection place the drug, as drawn?","o":["In the paraspinal muscle beside the spine","In the epidural fat outside the dura","In the venous blood of the portal system","In the CSF within the thecal sac"],"a":3,"e":"The diagram shows injection into the thecal sac, which holds the CSF around the spinal cord."},{"id":"L4-zhkefp","slide":128,"slides":[128],"q":"Where is an epidural injection placed, and what is it useful for?","o":["Into the CSF below the dura; useful in meningitis","Outside the dura mater; useful in obstetrics","Into the bone marrow cavity; useful in shock","Under the skin of the back; useful for slow release"],"a":1,"e":"Epidural injection is given just outside the dura mater, useful in obstetrics and inguinal hernia repair."},{"id":"L4-141azv9","slide":129,"slides":[129,130,131],"q":"Which patient positions are illustrated for epidural placement?","o":["Standing and walking","Lying and sitting","Prone only","Head-down only"],"a":1,"e":"The diagram slide shows lying position and sitting position."},{"id":"L4-ikp04m","slide":133,"slides":[133],"q":"When is the intraosseous route mainly practised?","o":["When the patient can swallow normally","When a slow continuous SC effect is wanted","When peripheral veins have collapsed","When a drug must reach the eye quickly"],"a":2,"e":"IO is used when accessible peripheral blood vessels have collapsed."},{"id":"L4-1wbr5ro","slide":136,"slides":[136],"q":"Intravitreal injection mainly delivers which drugs, and where?","o":["Anti-inflammatory drugs, into the vitreous humor","Antibiotics, into the cerebrospinal fluid space","Anaesthetics, into the epidural space of the spine","Vaccines, into the dermis of the forearm skin"],"a":0,"e":"Drugs, especially anti-inflammatory ones, are placed in the vitreous humor to treat deeper eye inflammation."},{"id":"L4-1l71ld4","slide":138,"slides":[138],"q":"Why is the intra-arterial route used in cancer chemotherapy?","o":["To localise the effect in one organ","To avoid the need for needles altogether","To slow drug absorption into the blood","To bypass the kidneys during excretion"],"a":0,"e":"Intra-arterial injection localises effect in specified organs; it is rarely used because of its dangers."},{"id":"L4-1y4gt3g","slide":139,"slides":[139],"q":"Why is the intraperitoneal route not used in humans?","o":["Risk of infection, organ damage and adhesions","The peritoneum has too small an absorbing surface","The drug is always destroyed by gastric acid","It needs no sterile technique"],"a":0,"e":"Intraperitoneal injection is used in laboratory animals; in humans it risks infection and damaging viscera."},{"id":"L4-q92pln","slide":140,"slides":[140],"q":"Which routes are the non-injection parenteral routes?","o":["Epidural, intrathecal and intravenous","Oral, buccal and rectal","Subcutaneous, intramuscular and intra-arterial","Topical, transdermal and inhalational"],"a":3,"e":"Non-injection (non-invasive) routes are topical, transdermal and inhalational."},{"id":"L4-1tbtiy5","slide":141,"slides":[141,142],"q":"Topical drugs on skin or mucosa are usually designed for what kind of effect?","o":["A systemic effect","A local effect","A first-pass effect","A depot effect"],"a":1,"e":"Drugs given topically (skin, nose, conjunctiva, vagina, bladder, urethra) are usually designed for local effects."},{"id":"L4-1mrlylf","slide":143,"slides":[143],"q":"How can timolol eye drops cause asthma symptoms in an asthmatic patient?","o":["Absorbed only through the skin of the eyelid margin itself","Acting only on the cornea, with no drainage whatsoever","Excreted in the tears before any absorption can occur","Drained via the lacrimal duct, swallowed and absorbed"],"a":3,"e":"Timolol drains via the lacrimal duct, is swallowed and absorbed, and its beta-blockade can trigger asthma."},{"id":"L4-dwkadj","slide":144,"slides":[144],"q":"Which duct drains eye drops towards the nose and mouth?","o":["Parotid salivary duct","Common bile duct","Thoracic lymph duct","Nasolacrimal duct"],"a":3,"e":"The diagram labels the inferior canaliculus and nasolacrimal duct."},{"id":"L4-csuo7c","slide":145,"slides":[145],"q":"Transdermal drugs are usually designed for which effect, and what raises absorption?","o":["A systemic effect; hydrated stratum corneum","A local effect; dryness of the stratum corneum","A local effect; thickening of the epidermal layer","A systemic effect; drying of the dermal layers"],"a":0,"e":"Increased hydration of the stratum corneum lowers resistance and increases absorption."},{"id":"L4-su8lee","slide":146,"slides":[146],"q":"What can an occlusive dressing do over a transdermal drug?","o":["Cool the skin and so stop all further absorption","Force the drug through the liver before the blood","Trap moisture, soften skin and raise absorption","Convert the patch into a deep intramuscular depot"],"a":2,"e":"Occlusion decreases water loss through perspiration, so the tissue macerates and absorption rises."},{"id":"L4-gx2wrd","slide":148,"slides":[148],"q":"Which drugs are given by inhalation, and why is absorption so rapid?","o":["Powdered solids only; a small and thick alveolar surface layer","Gases and volatile liquids; a large, thin alveolar lining","Solid tablets only; bronchi with very little blood flow today","Oily solutions only; a lipid-rich pleural membrane lining"],"a":1,"e":"Gaseous and volatile drugs, such as anaesthetics, are absorbed fast across the large, thin alveolar surface."}]},{"id":"L5","title":"Drug Distribution","sub":"Perfusion, reservoirs, protein binding, redistribution and Vd","emoji":"🩸","slides":[150,180],"recap":["Distribution is the movement of drug from circulation into tissues; only free (unbound) drug leaves the plasma. It depends on physicochemical properties, tissue perfusion and tissue binding.","Reservoirs (cells, fat, bone and teeth, plasma proteins) can prolong a drug's action or toxicity. Drugs bound to albumin can be displaced by others, as sulfonamides, ceftriaxone and excess vitamin K displace bilirubin in neonates and risk kernicterus.","Redistribution can end a drug's action: thiopental acts fast on the well-perfused brain, then moves into fat; repeated doses fill the stores and prolong the effect.","Volume of distribution (Vd = dose divided by plasma concentration) is an apparent volume that estimates tissue penetration and can exceed total body water."],"questions":[{"id":"L5-qq1irk","slide":150,"slides":[150],"q":"Which form of a drug in plasma can leave the circulation and enter the extravascular compartment?","o":["The free (unbound) form","The plasma protein-bound form","Both forms at the same rate","Neither form, since drugs stay in plasma"],"a":0,"e":"Only free drug diffuses into the extravascular compartment; protein-bound drug does not."},{"id":"L5-3x8b3f","slide":150,"slides":[150],"q":"By distribution, a drug reaches which destinations?","o":["Only its site of absorption, then the stomach wall","The kidney alone, where it is filtered into urine","Plasma alone, where it remains fully protein-bound","Action, storage, metabolism and elimination sites"],"a":3,"e":"Distribution takes the drug from circulation to interstitial then cellular fluid, reaching sites of action, storage, metabolism and elimination."},{"id":"L5-dj2gh","slide":152,"slides":[152],"q":"Which of these is NOT listed as a main factor affecting distribution?","o":["Organ or tissue perfusion","Route of administration","Tissue-binding capacity of the drug","Physicochemical properties of the drug"],"a":1,"e":"Distribution depends on physicochemical properties, perfusion and tissue binding."},{"id":"L5-72bh56","slide":153,"slides":[153],"q":"Which kind of drug distributes most easily into tissues?","o":["A large, water-soluble, highly ionised drug","A large, lipid-soluble, highly ionised drug","A small, lipid-soluble, non-ionised drug","A small, water-soluble, highly ionised drug"],"a":2,"e":"Lipid solubility, degree of ionisation and molecular size decide how easily a drug moves into tissues."},{"id":"L5-1wapvzv","slide":154,"slides":[154],"q":"Why do the kidneys, brain and lungs receive so much drug?","o":["They contain the most fat for drug storage","They hold the largest number of plasma proteins","They alone possess carrier proteins for drugs","They are very highly perfused organs"],"a":3,"e":"The amount of drug reaching an organ follows its share of cardiac output."},{"id":"L5-3ckwzx","slide":155,"slides":[155],"q":"Why do poorly perfused tissues show a slow onset of drug action?","o":["Poor perfusion makes the drug more lipid-soluble","Less drug is delivered, so equilibrium is slow","Poor perfusion speeds up metabolism in the tissue","Poor perfusion removes the blood-brain barrier there"],"a":1,"e":"Low perfusion delivers less drug, so tissue-plasma equilibrium takes a long time."},{"id":"L5-xi36ns","slide":156,"slides":[156],"q":"How does renal insufficiency prolong a drug's action?","o":["Less renal flow delivers less drug for elimination","More renal blood flow means the drug is eliminated faster","The kidney stores the drug as a depot in its cortex","The kidney converts the drug into an active prodrug"],"a":0,"e":"Reduced blood flow to the kidneys delivers less drug for elimination, extending therapeutic effects or causing toxicity."},{"id":"L5-w2rl32","slide":157,"slides":[157],"q":"What is a drug reservoir?","o":["A site where the drug is metabolised to inactive forms","A site where the drug is excreted from the body","A depot releasing free drug back into plasma","A receptor where the drug produces its effect"],"a":2,"e":"Reservoirs are depot sites from which free drug replenishes plasma."},{"id":"L5-che355","slide":157,"slides":[157],"q":"What is a potential consequence of drug reservoirs?","o":["Faster elimination of the drug","Prolonged drug activity or toxicity","Reduced bioavailability after injection","Loss of the drug's lipid solubility"],"a":1,"e":"Reservoirs are a potential source of prolonged activity or toxicity."},{"id":"L5-zjgiay","slide":158,"slides":[158],"q":"Which is NOT one of the types of drug reservoir named in the lecture?","o":["Fat (adipose tissue)","Bone and tooth stores","Renal tubular cells","Plasma protein binding"],"a":2,"e":"The reservoirs are cellular stores, fat, bone and teeth, and plasma protein binding."},{"id":"L5-1ij4gdh","slide":159,"slides":[159],"q":"Long-term quinacrine illustrates which kind of reservoir?","o":["A fat reservoir, with higher levels in adipose tissue","A cellular reservoir, concentrated in liver cells","A bone reservoir, with higher levels in the skeleton","A plasma protein reservoir bound to albumin sites"],"a":1,"e":"Quinacrine accumulates inside liver cells to levels several times those in plasma."},{"id":"L5-6xitez","slide":160,"slides":[160],"q":"Tissue binding of drugs usually involves which molecules?","o":["Bile salts, glucose and bilirubin pigments","Water, electrolytes and dissolved gases only","Collagen, keratin and elastin fibres alone","Proteins, phospholipids or nucleoproteins"],"a":3,"e":"Accumulation can be by active transport, and binding involves proteins, phospholipids or nucleoproteins."},{"id":"L5-1gucqfp","slide":161,"slides":[161],"q":"Why is fat a slow reservoir for lipid-soluble drugs?","o":["Fat tissue cannot dissolve any lipid-soluble drug","Blood flow to adipose tissue is slow","Fat is dense with receptors for all drugs","Fat tissue is the main site of drug excretion"],"a":1,"e":"Distribution into body fat is slow because blood flow to adipose tissue is slow."},{"id":"L5-6vjwna","slide":162,"slides":[162],"q":"What may happen to stored lipid-soluble drugs when body fat is drastically reduced, as in starvation?","o":["They are excreted unchanged at once in the urine","They are converted into inactive prodrug forms","They are mobilised with fat, prolonging action","They become bound permanently to bone mineral"],"a":2,"e":"Stored compounds can be mobilised with the fat, giving prolonged activity or toxic symptoms."},{"id":"L5-1k90rsk","slide":163,"slides":[163],"q":"Why do some drugs, such as tetracycline, concentrate in bone and teeth?","o":["They are chelated by calcium","They are bound to keratin in enamel","They are actively secreted by osteoblasts only","They dissolve in the marrow fat"],"a":0,"e":"Some drugs concentrate in bone because they are chelated by calcium."},{"id":"L5-upxm97","slide":163,"slides":[163],"q":"What may tetracycline cause in infants?","o":["Kernicterus","Phocomelia","Hepatic encephalopathy","Yellowish-brown teeth"],"a":3,"e":"Tetracycline deposits in developing teeth and discolours them."},{"id":"L5-4ei16w","slide":163,"slides":[163],"q":"Why can lead or radium in bone be dangerous over time?","o":["Bone slowly releases them, prolonging toxicity","Bone converts them into harmless metabolites","Bone excretes them rapidly and directly into urine","Bone binds them so tightly that they are inactive"],"a":0,"e":"Bone can become a source of slow release of toxic material, lengthening duration of toxicity."},{"id":"L5-1dynn27","slide":165,"slides":[165],"q":"How are bound and free drug in plasma related?","o":["They are two unrelated, permanent fractions","Free drug is converted to bound drug only in the liver","Bound drug is always excreted before free drug","They are in dynamic equilibrium by mass action"],"a":3,"e":"Plasma protein-bound drug is in dynamic equilibrium with free drug, as dictated by the law of mass action."},{"id":"L5-2pzurj","slide":166,"slides":[166],"q":"How does high protein binding affect a drug's action?","o":["Bound drug is more active than the free drug","Bound drug is cleared more quickly than free drug","Bound drug crosses the BBB more easily than free","Bound drug forms a reservoir, prolonging action"],"a":3,"e":"Protein-bound drug is a circulating reservoir that may prolong availability and action."},{"id":"L5-1rbu8jv","slide":167,"slides":[167],"q":"Why is a loading dose given for a drug with over 90% protein binding?","o":["To destroy the binding sites on albumin","To prevent the drug from reaching its receptor","To increase the drug's lipid solubility in plasma","To saturate albumin and tissue binding sites"],"a":3,"e":"A loading dose saturates albumin and tissue stores so the drug becomes therapeutically effective."},{"id":"L5-1sdjtur","slide":167,"slides":[167],"q":"What happens to free drug concentration in hypoalbuminaemia?","o":["It falls, so the drug's effects are much weaker","It stays unchanged because binding capacity is fixed","It rises only for water-soluble drugs, not others","It rises, so effects may be exaggerated and toxic"],"a":3,"e":"Fewer binding sites give more free drug and a possibly exaggerated reaction."},{"id":"L5-x62rgx","slide":168,"slides":[168],"q":"Two drugs compete for the same albumin binding sites. What is the likely consequence?","o":["Both drugs become fully bound and so inactive","Free levels of one or both drugs rise","Both drugs are excreted at once unchanged","Neither drug is affected by the competition"],"a":1,"e":"Binding sites are not substrate-specific; competition raises the free forms."},{"id":"L5-6dcgcf","slide":169,"slides":[169],"q":"Why is vitamin K given IM to newborns?","o":["To treat jaundice and prevent kernicterus","To prevent respiratory distress in the newborn","To stop infection of the umbilical stump","To prevent vitamin K deficiency bleeding"],"a":3,"e":"IM vitamin K prevents vitamin K deficiency bleeding (VKDB) in the neonate."},{"id":"L5-j87lsk","slide":169,"slides":[169],"q":"Which agents can raise the risk of kernicterus by displacing bilirubin?","o":["Sulfonamides, ceftriaxone and excess vitamin K","Penicillin, digoxin and excessive insulin doses","Morphine, atropine and neostigmine injections","Tetracycline, quinacrine and lead accumulation"],"a":0,"e":"Sulfonamides, ceftriaxone and excessive vitamin K displace bilirubin from plasma proteins."},{"id":"L5-7ghp04","slide":170,"slides":[170],"q":"Why can free bilirubin reach the infant's brain?","o":["The blood-brain barrier is immature","The infant brain has no blood supply at all","Bilirubin cannot be bound by albumin at all","Infants have far too much circulating albumin"],"a":0,"e":"Free bilirubin can enter the infant brain because the BBB is immature, causing kernicterus."},{"id":"L5-177fx8m","slide":170,"slides":[170],"q":"Why should sulfonamides be avoided in pregnant women near term?","o":["They damage the placenta and cause miscarriage","They cause tetracycline-like tooth staining","They displace bilirubin, risking kernicterus","They are excreted only in breast milk after birth"],"a":2,"e":"The lecture advises against sulfonamides in infants and pregnant women near term because of kernicterus."},{"id":"L5-x9id5a","slide":172,"slides":[172],"q":"What did the thalidomide disaster show about the placenta?","o":["It is completely impermeable to all drugs","It excludes only lipid-soluble drugs and gases","It does not exclude drugs from the fetus","It excretes drugs back to the mother at once"],"a":2,"e":"The old belief of an impermeable placenta was disproved; thalidomide caused congenital defects."},{"id":"L5-u8q3dj","slide":172,"slides":[172],"q":"In the 1960s, thalidomide was used in pregnant women as which type of drug?","o":["An antibiotic used to treat chest infections","An anticoagulant used to prevent blood clots","A hormone used as an oral contraceptive pill","A sedative/hypnotic used as an antiemetic"],"a":3,"e":"Thalidomide was a sedative/hypnotic also given in pregnancy as an antiemetic."},{"id":"L5-1qomued","slide":174,"slides":[174],"q":"Besides metabolism and excretion, how can a drug's action be terminated?","o":["By binding more strongly to its target receptors","By redistribution away from its site of action","By increasing its lipid solubility in the brain","By absorbing more of the drug from the gut wall"],"a":1,"e":"Termination can result from redistribution of the drug away from its site of action."},{"id":"L5-15azg6e","slide":175,"slides":[175],"q":"Why does IV thiopental act within 1-2 minutes?","o":["It is converted to an active metabolite in the liver","It is bound to albumin in the brain","High brain perfusion and high plasma concentration","It is absorbed slowly from fat"],"a":2,"e":"Thiopental is highly lipid soluble; the well-perfused brain meets a high plasma level."},{"id":"L5-15x66oo","slide":176,"slides":[176],"q":"Why is a single dose of thiopental short-acting?","o":["It leaves the brain for other tissues","It is rapidly excreted unchanged by the kidney","The brain metabolises it within a few minutes","The liver metabolises it before it reaches the brain"],"a":0,"e":"The brain does not store thiopental; plasma levels fall as the drug distributes to other tissues."},{"id":"L5-iz0opp","slide":177,"slides":[177],"q":"After repeated thiopental doses, why can plasma levels rise even after dosing stops?","o":["More of the drug is absorbed from the gut lumen","Stored drug is mobilised back into plasma","Albumin binding of the drug suddenly increases","The kidney stops excreting the drug entirely"],"a":1,"e":"Stored drug in tissue reservoirs is released into plasma again."},{"id":"L5-136q1q0","slide":178,"slides":[178],"q":"In whom does thiopental most often become long-acting after repeated doses?","o":["Obese patients","Underweight patients","Neonates only","Patients with raised urine pH"],"a":0,"e":"Fat acts as a large reservoir, prolonging the action of a short-acting drug, especially in the obese."},{"id":"L5-1h20wch","slide":179,"slides":[179],"q":"Apparent volume of distribution is calculated as:","o":["Plasma drug concentration divided by dose","Dose divided by plasma drug concentration","Dose multiplied by half-life","Clearance divided by half-life"],"a":1,"e":"Vd (apparent) = dose / plasma drug concentration."},{"id":"L5-cn5zto","slide":179,"slides":[179],"q":"A 500 mg dose gives a plasma concentration of 10 mg/L. What is the apparent Vd?","o":["50 L","5 L","0.02 L","510 L"],"a":0,"e":"Vd = dose / concentration = 500 / 10 = 50 L."},{"id":"L5-195svay","slide":179,"slides":[179],"q":"Why is Vd called 'apparent'?","o":["It can only be visualised on an imaging scan","It is measured only for drugs with a visible effect","It does not represent any real physical volume","It is calculated from the daily urine volume passed"],"a":2,"e":"Vd is a calculated volume if the drug were uniformly distributed, not an actual space."},{"id":"L5-as96ri","slide":180,"slides":[180],"q":"A drug has a Vd far greater than total body water. What does this suggest?","o":["It remains confined to the plasma compartment","It is excreted before it can distribute","It is distributed widely into the tissues","It is entirely bound to albumin within plasma"],"a":2,"e":"Vd estimates tissue penetration and can exceed total body fluid when the drug goes into other compartments."}]},{"id":"L6","title":"Drug Biotransformation","sub":"Phase I and II reactions, enzymes and factors affecting metabolism","emoji":"🧬","slides":[181,201],"recap":["Biotransformation turns lipid-soluble drugs into more polar metabolites so they can be excreted. Its purpose is polarity, not simply inactivation.","Phase I (functionalisation) uses oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis, cyclisation or decyclisation; Phase II (conjugation) couples drug to acetic acid, sulfate, glucuronic acid, glutathione, methyl groups or glycine.","Microsomal enzymes (smooth ER, mainly liver; e.g. cytochrome P450, glucuronyl transferase) and non-microsomal enzymes (plasma, cytosol, mitochondria etc.) do the work.","Genetics (fast vs slow acetylators for isoniazid), age, sex and environment (smoking, pesticides causing enzyme induction) change the rate of metabolism."],"questions":[{"id":"L6-rd6w5v","slide":181,"slides":[181],"q":"Why would a lipid-soluble drug stay in the body if it were not metabolised?","o":["It is never filtered across the glomerulus at all","It is secreted by the tubules far too quickly","It is broken down by urine within the bladder","It is filtered, then reabsorbed by the tubules"],"a":3,"e":"Without biotransformation, filtered lipid-soluble drug is reabsorbed, prolonging its stay."},{"id":"L6-17sge4n","slide":182,"slides":[182],"q":"Biotransformation is best defined as the conversion of drugs to:","o":["More lipid-soluble forms that cross membranes easily","Larger molecules that the kidney cannot filter","Prodrugs that persist in the body much longer","More polar metabolites that are easier to excrete"],"a":3,"e":"Biotransformation raises water solubility so that elimination is effective."},{"id":"L6-1x2al1s","slide":183,"slides":[183],"q":"Which statement about the purpose of biotransformation is correct?","o":["It aims to make the drug more polar for elimination","It always makes the drug completely inactive in the body","It always makes the drug more toxic to the liver","It aims to make the drug more lipid-soluble for storage"],"a":0,"e":"The sole purpose is to convert drugs into more polar, readily eliminated metabolites."},{"id":"L6-orffm7","slide":184,"slides":[184],"q":"How do Phase I reactions make a drug more polar?","o":["By attaching glucuronic acid or sulfate to the drug","By removing every polar group from the molecule","By binding the drug tightly to plasma albumin","By unmasking or adding groups such as -OH or -NH2"],"a":3,"e":"Phase I (functionalisation) unmasks or introduces electron-rich polar functional groups."},{"id":"L6-gpf0mv","slide":185,"slides":[185],"q":"Which of these is a Phase I process?","o":["Glucuronidation","Acetylation","Sulfation","Hydrolysis"],"a":3,"e":"Phase I processes are oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis, cyclisation and decyclisation."},{"id":"L6-1m8v469","slide":185,"slides":[185],"q":"What may happen to a drug after Phase I reactions?","o":["It must always be completely inactivated first","It may be excreted, or enter Phase II","It must always be reabsorbed in the tubules again","It is deposited in bone and kept there for life"],"a":1,"e":"Some drugs are polar enough to leave after Phase I; others need Phase II."},{"id":"L6-1uhqglj","slide":186,"slides":[186],"q":"Phase II reactions are described as:","o":["Breakdown reactions that split the drug in two","Reactions that remove polar groups from the drug molecule","Reactions that take place only in the kidney","Conjugation reactions with endogenous substances"],"a":3,"e":"Phase II couples the drug or its metabolites with endogenous substances to make them polar."},{"id":"L6-p58siv","slide":187,"slides":[187],"q":"Which pair correctly matches a Phase II reaction with its endogenous substance?","o":["Methylation with glycine residues","Acetylation with sulfate groups","Sulfation with glutathione conjugates","Glucuronidation with glucuronic acid"],"a":3,"e":"Acetic acid gives acetylation, sulfate gives sulfation, glucuronic acid gives glucuronidation, glutathione gives conjugation and glycine gives glycine conjugation."},{"id":"L6-o8n3fy","slide":188,"slides":[188],"q":"What does the reverse sequential pattern of biotransformation mean?","o":["Phase I happens before Phase II","Both phases happen at once","Phase II happens before Phase I","Only Phase I happens"],"a":2,"e":"Patterns include sequential (I then II), reverse (II then I), parallel and single-phase pathways."},{"id":"L6-j0dal5","slide":189,"slides":[189],"q":"Which of these is a microsomal enzyme system?","o":["Plasma esterase","Cytochrome P450","Plasma amidase","Flavoprotein oxidase"],"a":1,"e":"Microsomal enzymes include glucuronyl transferase, monooxygenases and cytochrome P450."},{"id":"L6-1oge6am","slide":190,"slides":[190],"q":"Which of these is a non-microsomal enzyme?","o":["Cytochrome P450","Glucuronyl transferase","Esterase","Monooxygenase"],"a":2,"e":"Flavoproteins, amidases and esterases are non-microsomal enzymes."},{"id":"L6-34vqkg","slide":191,"slides":[191],"q":"Where are microsomal enzymes located?","o":["In the smooth ER, mainly in the liver","In the nucleus of every body cell type","In the plasma of circulating blood only","In the mitochondria of skeletal muscle cells"],"a":0,"e":"Microsomal enzymes sit in the sER of the liver, GIT, lungs and kidneys."},{"id":"L6-je9dgf","slide":192,"slides":[192],"q":"Non-microsomal enzymes are NOT found in which location?","o":["Plasma of the blood","The cytosol of cells","The mitochondria","The smooth ER"],"a":3,"e":"Non-microsomal enzymes occur in plasma, cytosol, lysosomes, mitochondria and the nuclear membrane."},{"id":"L6-acte2g","slide":193,"slides":[193],"q":"Unexpectedly high isoniazid levels in some patients are explained by:","o":["Genetic polymorphism in metabolism","Poor tablet formulation in all patients","Expired tablets issued to every patient","A larger dose prescribed by mistake each time"],"a":0,"e":"Metabolism of drugs may be affected by the genetic make-up of the individual."},{"id":"L6-1k3a0yg","slide":194,"slides":[194],"q":"Isoniazid is metabolised in the liver by which process, and how is the population distributed?","o":["N-acetylation; fast and slow acetylators","Glucuronidation; a single normal distribution","Oxidation; a bimodal distribution by age","Sulfation; a single distribution by sex"],"a":0,"e":"Isoniazid is N-acetylated; populations split into fast and slow acetylators."},{"id":"L6-1ej3ywt","slide":195,"slides":[195],"q":"Which toxicity is linked to slow acetylators of isoniazid?","o":["Hepatotoxicity from acetylhydrazine build-up","Peripheral neuropathy from the parent drug","Kernicterus from displaced free bilirubin","Hepatic encephalopathy from raised ammonia"],"a":1,"e":"Slow acetylators accumulate the parent compound, causing peripheral neuropathy."},{"id":"L6-wrflcq","slide":195,"slides":[195],"q":"Which toxicity is linked to fast acetylators of isoniazid?","o":["Peripheral neuropathy from the parent drug","Malignant hyperthermia","Hepatotoxicity from acetylhydrazine","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"a":2,"e":"Fast acetylators produce more of the acetylated metabolite, giving hepatotoxicity."},{"id":"L6-1949v4m","slide":196,"slides":[196],"q":"Which drug also shows fast and slow acetylator behaviour like isoniazid?","o":["Benzylpenicillin","Morphine sulfate","Hydralazine","Atropine sulfate"],"a":2,"e":"Hydralazine and procainamide show a similar phenomenon."},{"id":"L6-an4bsg","slide":197,"slides":[197],"q":"Which age groups are most susceptible to the effects and toxicity of drugs?","o":["The very young and the very old","Teenagers and young adults","Middle-aged adults only","All ages are equally susceptible"],"a":0,"e":"Individuals at the extremes of life are more susceptible, as metabolism varies with age."},{"id":"L6-1gktqii","slide":199,"slides":[199],"q":"Why is drug metabolism slower at the extremes of age?","o":["Increased enzyme activity and excess cofactors","Greater lipid solubility of every drug given","Lower enzyme activity or fewer cofactors","More rapid renal elimination of metabolites"],"a":2,"e":"Slow metabolism reflects lower oxidising-enzyme activity or fewer endogenous cofactors, plus immature or ageing organs."},{"id":"L6-odcgjf","slide":200,"slides":[200],"q":"What does the lecture say about gender and metabolism of some drugs?","o":["Males and females metabolise every drug identically","Female rats metabolise all drugs faster than males","Gender alters only drug excretion, not metabolism","Young female rats metabolise some drugs more slowly"],"a":3,"e":"Differences are thought to be due to hormonal constituents of the two genders."},{"id":"L6-dg2qsh","slide":201,"slides":[201],"q":"Why may smokers and industrial workers exposed to pesticides metabolise some drugs faster?","o":["Their enzymes are inhibited by smoke and chemicals","Their habit or work leads to enzyme induction","They excrete drugs much more quickly in sweat","They absorb less of each drug from the gut"],"a":1,"e":"Smoking and pesticide exposure tend to induce drug-metabolising enzymes."},{"id":"L6-1f9kx9e","slide":198,"slides":[198],"q":"Which statement about age and pharmacokinetics is supported by the lecture?","o":["Metabolism is identical from birth to old age","Only excretion varies with age","Metabolism of drugs tends to vary with age","Age affects only drug absorption"],"a":2,"e":"Pharmacokinetics, especially metabolism, varies with age and tends to be slow at the extremes."}]},{"id":"L7","title":"Drug Excretion","sub":"Renal, biliary, pulmonary and other routes of elimination","emoji":"🫘","slides":[202,226],"recap":["Elimination moves the drug or its metabolites out of the body; the kidney is the most important organ, while lungs clear volatile drugs and can excrete polar and non-polar drugs.","Urinary excretion = glomerular filtration + tubular secretion - tubular reabsorption. Only free drug is filtered; secretion is carrier-mediated and competitive (probenecid prolongs penicillin).","Urine pH changes reabsorption of weak acids and bases: acidify to clear weak bases, alkalinise to clear weak acids. In renal insufficiency, half-life rises, so reduce dose or widen the interval.","Bile carries drugs into the gut; enterohepatic circulation reabsorbs them, raising half-life (digoxin 40 h, digitoxin 160 h). Milk is a route of risk to babies."],"questions":[{"id":"L7-ey4ade","slide":202,"slides":[202],"q":"Which excretory organ clears both polar and non-polar drugs efficiently?","o":["The kidneys","The liver","The lungs","The salivary glands"],"a":2,"e":"Polar drugs are excreted efficiently by most organs, except lungs, which handle both polar and non-polar drugs."},{"id":"L7-1gw96px","slide":202,"slides":[202],"q":"Drug elimination is best defined as:","o":["Movement of the drug from plasma into the tissues","Removal of drug or metabolites from the body","Conversion of the active drug into an inactive prodrug","Absorption of the drug from the gut into circulation"],"a":1,"e":"Elimination moves parent drug or metabolites from the body to the external environment."},{"id":"L7-1emrccn","slide":203,"slides":[203],"q":"Which is the most important excretory organ for drugs?","o":["The mammary glands","The skin","The lacrimal glands","The kidneys"],"a":3,"e":"The kidneys are the principal excretory organ, followed by liver, lungs and others."},{"id":"L7-p4ro02","slide":204,"slides":[204],"q":"About what share of cardiac output goes to the kidneys?","o":["About 5%","About 50%","About 25%","About 75%"],"a":2,"e":"The kidney receives about 25% of cardiac output, and nephrons enhance its excretory ability."},{"id":"L7-11bheax","slide":205,"slides":[205],"q":"In the nephron diagram, where does active secretion of drugs occur?","o":["The afferent arteriole","The proximal tubule","The efferent arteriole","The collecting vein"],"a":1,"e":"The diagram labels active secretion at the proximal tubule, with passive reabsorption further along."},{"id":"L7-vpni5r","slide":206,"slides":[206],"q":"Which substances are excreted in the urine?","o":["Only metabolites and never the unchanged parent drug","Only highly lipid-soluble drugs that cross membranes","Only drugs that are bound tightly to albumin","Polar drugs, metabolites and many unchanged drugs"],"a":3,"e":"Polar drugs and metabolites are excreted in urine, and many drugs leave virtually unchanged."},{"id":"L7-nwbiy5","slide":207,"slides":[207],"q":"Which two processes bring a drug into the renal tubule?","o":["Tubular reabsorption and urination","Glomerular filtration and tubular secretion","Glomerular filtration and tubular reabsorption","Tubular secretion and urination"],"a":1,"e":"Filtration and secretion bring drug in; reabsorption and urination take drug out."},{"id":"L7-69gao2","slide":208,"slides":[208],"q":"How is the amount of drug excreted in urine (U) calculated?","o":["U = GF - TS + TR","U = TS + TR - GF","U = GF + TS - TR","U = GF x TS x TR"],"a":2,"e":"What enters the tubules (GF + TS) equals what leaves (TR + U), so U = GF + TS - TR."},{"id":"L7-fheha9","slide":208,"slides":[208],"q":"If tubular reabsorption of a drug increases while the other processes stay the same, what happens to urinary excretion?","o":["It rises","It falls","It stays the same","It doubles"],"a":1,"e":"Since U = GF + TS - TR, more reabsorption means less drug excreted."},{"id":"L7-1p6420w","slide":209,"slides":[209],"q":"Which drug is NOT filtered at the glomerulus?","o":["A protein-bound drug","A free drug of low molecular weight","A free water-soluble drug","A free metabolite of the drug"],"a":0,"e":"Drug bound to protein is not filtered; filtration passes drugs of molecular weight below 68,000."},{"id":"L7-1n1b6kl","slide":209,"slides":[209],"q":"Glomerular filtration of a drug is:","o":["Active, selective and energy-dependent saturable","Passive, non-selective and non-saturable","Passive, selective and saturable by carriers","Active, non-selective and energy-requiring"],"a":1,"e":"Filtration is passive, non-selective and non-saturable, driven by the GFR."},{"id":"L7-ot0fs1","slide":210,"slides":[210],"q":"What determines the filtration pressure that sets GFR?","o":["Urine pH, drug pKa and tubular flow rate","Renal blood flow and arteriolar resistance","Bile flow rate and gut transit time","Drug lipid solubility and molecular size only"],"a":1,"e":"Filtration pressure depends on renal blood flow and afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance."},{"id":"L7-17b7gdh","slide":211,"slides":[211],"q":"Which combination raises filtration pressure and drug excretion by filtration?","o":["Afferent constriction with efferent dilatation","Afferent dilatation with efferent constriction","Afferent and efferent dilatation together","Afferent and efferent constriction together"],"a":1,"e":"Lower afferent resistance and higher efferent resistance raise filtration pressure and GFR."},{"id":"L7-15ldqeq","slide":211,"slides":[211],"q":"Why does extensive plasma protein binding reduce glomerular filtration of a drug?","o":["Bound drug is filtered faster","Protein binding raises GFR","Only free drug is filtered","Bound drug is secreted into bile first"],"a":2,"e":"Excretion by filtration depends on the free plasma drug concentration."},{"id":"L7-54xx7i","slide":212,"slides":[212],"q":"Which description fits tubular secretion?","o":["Passive, unidirectional and highly substrate-selective","Energy-free and restricted to lipid-soluble drugs only","Carrier-mediated, bidirectional and non-specific","Dependent solely on urine pH and plasma protein binding"],"a":2,"e":"Both active and facilitated transport are involved; the systems are bidirectional and non-specific."},{"id":"L7-kugbdk","slide":213,"slides":[213],"q":"When drugs and endogenous substances compete for the tubular carriers, what happens?","o":["Endogenous substances are secreted; the drug is retained","The drug is secreted faster than the endogenous substance","Neither the drug nor the endogenous substance is secreted","Both are diverted into the bile instead of the urine"],"a":0,"e":"In the presence of competitors, endogenous substances are largely secreted into the tubule and exogenous ones mainly retained."},{"id":"L7-gq0cw7","slide":215,"slides":[215],"q":"Why is probenecid given with penicillin?","o":["It speeds up renal tubular secretion of penicillin into urine","It makes penicillin more lipid-soluble for deeper tissue entry","It prevents penicillin binding to albumin in the plasma","It competes for secretion, prolonging penicillin's action"],"a":3,"e":"Competition retains penicillin and increases its T1/2, so its therapeutic effect is prolonged."},{"id":"L7-xolluw","slide":214,"slides":[214],"q":"Drugs and endogenous substances with similar properties compete for the same tubular mechanisms. Which outcome does the lecture describe?","o":["Endogenous substances are secreted; the drug is retained","The drug is largely secreted; endogenous substances are retained","Both are secreted equally into the proximal tubule lumen","Both are retained in the blood until filtration is complete"],"a":0,"e":"In the presence of competitors, endogenous substances are largely secreted into the renal tubule while exogenous substances are mainly retained."},{"id":"L7-ywlnm4","slide":215,"slides":[215],"q":"Probenecid with uric acid has what result?","o":["Competition increases the elimination of uric acid","Competition retains uric acid in the body","Competition converts uric acid into penicillin","Competition blocks glomerular filtration of uric acid"],"a":0,"e":"The slide pairs probenecid and uric acid to raise uric acid elimination."},{"id":"L7-7kely3","slide":216,"slides":[216],"q":"Why does a lipid-soluble drug diffuse back out of the tubule?","o":["Carrier proteins pump it back into the plasma using ATP","It binds to albumin inside the tubule and is released","It is filtered again by the glomerulus after secretion","Water reabsorption concentrates it, creating a gradient"],"a":3,"e":"Reabsorption of water and sodium concentrates solute, creating a gradient for passive diffusion."},{"id":"L7-vgp93s","slide":216,"slides":[216],"q":"The extent of tubular reabsorption of a weak electrolyte depends on:","o":["Urine pH and the pKa of the drug","Plasma albumin and the patient's body weight","Biliary flow rate and intestinal pH","Dose size and urine temperature"],"a":0,"e":"Permeation depends on urine pH and drug pKa, per Henderson-Hasselbalch."},{"id":"L7-17lxegv","slide":217,"slides":[217],"q":"To hasten elimination of a weak basic drug, urine should be:","o":["Alkalinised, e.g. with sodium bicarbonate or citrate","Left unchanged while increasing fluid intake","Concentrated by restricting fluid intake","Acidified, e.g. with ascorbic acid or NH4Cl"],"a":3,"e":"Acidification ionises weak bases in the tubule, trapping them for excretion."},{"id":"L7-rz6hw","slide":217,"slides":[217],"q":"Which urine change helps eliminate a weak acidic drug?","o":["Alkalinisation","Acidification","Neither, pH never matters","Concentrating the urine"],"a":0,"e":"By the same principle, alkalinisation ionises weak acids and traps them in urine."},{"id":"L7-12jku9d","slide":217,"slides":[217],"q":"What caution applies to acidifying urine with NH4Cl?","o":["It always causes a dangerous rise in blood pressure","Acidosis may cause cardiac and other problems","It makes most drugs more lipid-soluble in tubules","It reduces glomerular filtration in the nephron"],"a":1,"e":"The lecture cautions that acidosis can cause cardiac problems."},{"id":"L7-1c9065y","slide":218,"slides":[218],"q":"Half-life (T1/2) is the time taken for:","o":["A single dose to be completely absorbed","A drug to reach its maximum plasma peak","Plasma concentration to fall by half","Half of the dose to be bound to protein"],"a":2,"e":"Half-life is the time for the plasma drug concentration to be reduced by half."},{"id":"L7-e0kyxo","slide":218,"slides":[218],"q":"Clearance is expressed as:","o":["Plasma volume cleared of drug per minute per kg","Amount of drug excreted in the urine per dose given","Time taken for the plasma drug level to fall by half","Dose divided by the apparent volume of distribution"],"a":0,"e":"Clearance (ml/min/kg) is the plasma volume cleared per unit time per unit body weight."},{"id":"L7-c56zzj","slide":218,"slides":[218],"q":"A drug's plasma level is 80 mg/L. After one half-life, what is the expected level?","o":["20 mg/L","60 mg/L","0 mg/L","40 mg/L"],"a":3,"e":"One half-life halves the concentration: 80 to 40 mg/L."},{"id":"L7-1ky1eyl","slide":219,"slides":[219],"q":"How should dosing change in renal insufficiency?","o":["Increase the dose and shorten the interval","Reduce the dose or widen the dosing interval","Give the same dose but more often each day","Switch to the rectal route to avoid renal clearance"],"a":1,"e":"Insufficiency lengthens T1/2, so reduce dose and decrease frequency to avoid toxicity."},{"id":"L7-1a30zv","slide":220,"slides":[220],"q":"Which two pathways does biliary excretion of a drug follow?","o":["Filtered at the glomerulus, then secreted along the proximal tubule","Exhaled unchanged by the lungs, then excreted in sweat and saliva","Secreted in saliva, then swallowed and absorbed from the stomach","Unchanged, or biotransformed first, then bile to gut and faeces"],"a":3,"e":"Drugs enter hepatocytes and are secreted into bile, either unchanged or after biotransformation."},{"id":"L7-1vd7d5p","slide":221,"slides":[221],"q":"Biliary carrier systems exist for which groups of substances?","o":["Only water, electrolytes and simple sugars","Acidic drugs, basic drugs and steroids","Only large proteins and dietary fats","Only fat-soluble vitamins and trace metals"],"a":1,"e":"Separate carrier mechanisms serve acidic drugs, basic drugs and steroids, with no substrate selectivity."},{"id":"L7-1fsvngj","slide":222,"slides":[222],"q":"What allows a glucuronide-conjugated drug to be reabsorbed from the gut?","o":["Further conjugation with sulfate in the intestinal wall","Binding to bile salts, then absorption in the colon","Filtration back through the glomerulus into plasma","Gut-flora deconjugation into a lipid-soluble product"],"a":3,"e":"Deconjugation in the GIT yields lipid-soluble products that are reabsorbed."},{"id":"L7-i291y4","slide":223,"slides":[223],"q":"What does enterohepatic circulation do to a drug's half-life and duration of action?","o":["Lengthens both","Shortens both","Shortens half-life but lengthens duration","Leaves both unchanged"],"a":0,"e":"It raises T1/2, redistribution and reduces excretion, so action is prolonged."},{"id":"L7-uza043","slide":223,"slides":[223],"q":"Which has the longer half-life per the lecture?","o":["Digoxin, about 40 hours","Both about equal at 40 hours","Both about equal at 160 hours","Digitoxin, about 160 hours"],"a":3,"e":"T1/2 of digoxin is 40 h and digitoxin 160 h."},{"id":"L7-1h9p005","slide":224,"slides":[224],"q":"Which drugs are excreted mainly by the lungs?","o":["Polar metabolites that are tightly bound to albumin","Volatile drugs, e.g. alcohol and anaesthetics","Heavy metals that are chelated and stored in bone","Large peptide drugs and protein hormones such as insulin"],"a":1,"e":"Pulmonary excretion is important for volatile drugs and follows Fick's law."},{"id":"L7-anh9n6","slide":225,"slides":[225],"q":"Why can morphine poisoning be eliminated through the faeces?","o":["Morphine is an acid and is trapped in the stomach","Morphine is metabolised to a lipid-soluble product in the stomach","Weak bases are ion-trapped in the acidic stomach","Morphine is filtered by the stomach wall"],"a":2,"e":"Weak basic drugs that penetrate the acid stomach are ionised and trapped, then lost in faeces."},{"id":"L7-rqohut","slide":226,"slides":[226],"q":"Which excretion route poses a risk to infants of treated mothers?","o":["Sweat and sebum secretions","Breast milk","Tears and lacrimal fluid","Saliva and gastric secretions"],"a":1,"e":"Drugs in the milk of nursing mothers can put the baby at risk."}]},{"id":"L8","title":"Adrenergic Pharmacology","sub":"Noradrenaline transmission, adrenoceptors, agonists and antagonists","emoji":"⚡","slides":[227,264],"recap":["Noradrenaline is made from tyrosine (tyrosine hydroxylase is rate-limiting), stored in vesicles by VMAT, released by Ca2+-dependent exocytosis, and removed mainly by NET reuptake and MAO/COMT breakdown.","Alpha-1 causes vasoconstriction; alpha-2 inhibits transmitter release; beta-1 raises cardiac rate and force; beta-2 gives bronchodilatation and vasodilatation; beta-3 gives lipolysis.","Agonists can be direct (adrenaline), indirect (amphetamine, cocaine, tyramine) or mixed (ephedrine). Key uses: adrenaline in cardiac arrest and anaphylaxis, dobutamine in cardiogenic shock, salbutamol in asthma.","Alpha-blockers (prazosin, tamsulosin) treat hypertension and BPH; beta-blockers treat angina, MI, hypertension, glaucoma and more, but risk bronchoconstriction, bradycardia and heart failure."],"questions":[{"id":"L8-1pfphzg","slide":232,"slides":[232],"q":"Which enzyme catalyses the rate-limiting step of noradrenaline synthesis?","o":["Tyrosine hydroxylase","DOPA decarboxylase","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase"],"a":0,"e":"L-tyrosine to DOPA by tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting step and occurs only in catecholaminergic neurons."},{"id":"L8-78gt9q","slide":232,"slides":[232],"q":"Where is dopamine beta-hydroxylase located, and what does it make?","o":["In the cytosol; it converts DOPA to dopamine first","In the adrenal medulla only; it makes adrenaline","In vesicles; it converts dopamine to noradrenaline","In the neuron membrane; it breaks down noradrenaline"],"a":2,"e":"DBH sits in the vesicles and converts dopamine to noradrenaline."},{"id":"L8-1d5uu70","slide":232,"slides":[232],"q":"Which enzyme converts noradrenaline to adrenaline in the adrenal medulla?","o":["Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase within the vesicles","Tyrosine hydroxylase in the nerve terminal","Monoamine oxidase on mitochondrial membranes"],"a":0,"e":"PNMT adds a methyl group to noradrenaline to give adrenaline."},{"id":"L8-ycmxgy","slide":233,"slides":[233],"q":"In the synthesis scheme, which inhibitor blocks dopamine beta-hydroxylase?","o":["Carbidopa","Alpha-methyltyrosine","Fusaric acid","Reserpine"],"a":2,"e":"Alpha-methyltyrosine blocks tyrosine hydroxylase, carbidopa blocks DOPA decarboxylase and fusaric acid blocks DBH."},{"id":"L8-19g4kwe","slide":234,"slides":[234],"q":"In the noradrenaline diagram, which drugs block release of the transmitter?","o":["Reserpine and tetrabenazine","Cocaine and amitriptyline","Bretylium, guanethidine","Carbidopa and alpha-methyltyrosine"],"a":2,"e":"Reserpine blocks vesicle uptake; guanethidine and bretylium block release; cocaine and tricyclics block reuptake."},{"id":"L8-auayqb","slide":235,"slides":[235],"q":"Which transporter loads noradrenaline into vesicles, and which drug blocks it?","o":["NET, blocked by cocaine","VMAT, blocked by guanethidine","NET, blocked by reserpine","VMAT, blocked by reserpine"],"a":3,"e":"The vesicular monoamine transporter is reserpine-sensitive."},{"id":"L8-ksmi93","slide":235,"slides":[235],"q":"Why is the cytosolic noradrenaline concentration normally low?","o":["It is pumped out through the NET at once","It is tightly bound to ATP in the cytosol","It is converted back to dopamine at once","MAO in nerve terminals breaks it down"],"a":3,"e":"MAO in the nerve terminals keeps cytosolic noradrenaline low."},{"id":"L8-hzi4wq","slide":236,"slides":[236],"q":"What is the main way noradrenaline's action is terminated?","o":["Hydrolysis by plasma acetylcholinesterase","Excretion unchanged in the urine via kidneys","Reuptake via the NET into terminals","Diffusion into the bloodstream and liver"],"a":2,"e":"Action ends mainly by reuptake of noradrenaline through the NET transporter."},{"id":"L8-mnolqg","slide":236,"slides":[236,237],"q":"How do indirectly acting amines such as amphetamine release noradrenaline?","o":["They displace it from vesicles so it leaks via NET","They trigger Ca2+-dependent exocytosis from vesicles","They block MAO in the cytosol and nerve terminals","They activate the beta receptor on the terminal"],"a":0,"e":"Non-exocytotic release occurs when amphetamine displaces noradrenaline from vesicles and it leaks out through NET."},{"id":"L8-ipp22o","slide":238,"slides":[238],"q":"How does methyldopa lower blood pressure?","o":["It becomes a false transmitter that is an alpha-2 agonist","It blocks tyrosine hydroxylase to cut noradrenaline synthesis","It blocks the NET reuptake transporter in the terminals","It blocks beta-1 receptors in the heart and kidney"],"a":0,"e":"Methyldopa becomes methylnoradrenaline, which strongly activates presynaptic alpha-2 receptors."},{"id":"L8-foaan5","slide":238,"slides":[238],"q":"Carbidopa blocks DOPA decarboxylase. What is its clinical use?","o":["Resistant hypertension","Parkinson's disease","Chronic bronchial asthma","Narrow-angle glaucoma"],"a":1,"e":"Carbidopa is used in Parkinson's disease and has little effect on noradrenaline synthesis."},{"id":"L8-1ddokm5","slide":239,"slides":[239],"q":"What serious adverse effect can reserpine cause?","o":["Bronchospasm","Severe depression","Hyperglycaemia","Raised intraocular pressure"],"a":1,"e":"Reserpine depletes noradrenaline stores and may cause severe depression; it is clinically obsolete."},{"id":"L8-15dhjy5","slide":239,"slides":[239],"q":"Why are neuron-blocking drugs such as guanethidine little used now?","o":["They have no useful effect on blood pressure at all","They cause postural hypotension and diarrhoea","They are destroyed by gastric acid before absorption","They cause marked bronchodilatation and tachycardia"],"a":1,"e":"Guanethidine and bethanidine are effective but their side effects limit use."},{"id":"L8-1fzpumf","slide":240,"slides":[240],"q":"Why can a patient on an MAO inhibitor get severe hypertension after eating tyramine-rich food?","o":["Tyramine blocks beta receptors and raises heart rate","Tyramine only stops reuptake of noradrenaline at the NET","Tyramine inhibits release of noradrenaline from vesicles","Tyramine displaces noradrenaline that MAO cannot clear"],"a":3,"e":"Indirectly acting amines displace noradrenaline; MAO inhibition greatly enhances this effect."},{"id":"L8-vu88y9","slide":240,"slides":[240],"q":"What is 6-hydroxydopamine used for?","o":["Treating resistant hypertension in the clinic setting","Experimental destruction of sympathetic nerves","Treating acute severe asthma in emergency settings","Dilating the pupil before routine eye examinations"],"a":1,"e":"It is taken up and converted to a toxic metabolite, so it is used experimentally, not clinically."},{"id":"L8-xi9tim","slide":241,"slides":[241],"q":"Which pair of drugs is used to treat attention deficit hyperactivity disorder?","o":["Reserpine and carbidopa-levodopa","Prazosin and phenoxybenzamine","Methylphenidate, atomoxetine","Propranolol and carvedilol"],"a":2,"e":"Indirectly acting CNS stimulants methylphenidate and atomoxetine are used in ADHD."},{"id":"L8-69g8oy","slide":241,"slides":[241],"q":"What do NET inhibitors such as cocaine and tricyclic antidepressants do to sympathetic effects?","o":["Abolish them","Leave them unchanged","Enhance them","Reverse them"],"a":2,"e":"Blocking reuptake leaves more noradrenaline in the synapse, so sympathetic effects increase."},{"id":"L8-100ezis","slide":243,"slides":[243],"q":"Which statement about adrenoceptors is correct?","o":["All are G-protein-coupled membrane receptors","Alpha are ion channels while beta are enzymes","There are only two subtypes, alpha and beta","They are located only in the heart and vessels"],"a":0,"e":"There are alpha-1 and alpha-2 (each with three subtypes) and beta-1 to beta-3, all G-protein-coupled."},{"id":"L8-pbks4v","slide":244,"slides":[244],"q":"Which effect follows stimulation of alpha-1 receptors?","o":["Bronchodilatation","Increased heart rate","Lipolysis","Vasoconstriction"],"a":3,"e":"Alpha-1 effects include vasoconstriction, relaxation of GI muscle, salivary secretion and glycogenolysis."},{"id":"L8-1asfle4","slide":244,"slides":[244],"q":"Which effect follows stimulation of alpha-2 receptors?","o":["Inhibition of transmitter release","Increased cardiac rate and force","Relaxation of bronchial smooth muscle","Increased insulin release from islets"],"a":0,"e":"Alpha-2 activation inhibits noradrenaline and acetylcholine release, promotes platelet aggregation and inhibits insulin release."},{"id":"L8-kml6bu","slide":245,"slides":[245],"q":"Which effect follows stimulation of beta-2 receptors?","o":["Increased cardiac force","Lipolysis","Bronchodilatation","Vasoconstriction"],"a":2,"e":"Beta-2 gives bronchodilatation, vasodilatation, visceral relaxation, glycogenolysis and tremor."},{"id":"L8-ykcr0b","slide":245,"slides":[245],"q":"Which pair of effects matches beta-1 and beta-3 receptors, in that order?","o":["Lipolysis; increased cardiac rate and force","Bronchodilatation; lipolysis","Vasodilatation; increased cardiac force","Increased cardiac rate and force; lipolysis"],"a":3,"e":"Beta-1 acts on the heart; beta-3 causes lipolysis."},{"id":"L8-1ek2mlr","slide":248,"slides":[248],"q":"Which of these is a catecholamine?","o":["Phenylephrine","Ephedrine","Amphetamine","Isoprenaline"],"a":3,"e":"Catecholamines have a 3,4-dihydroxybenzene group: noradrenaline, adrenaline, isoprenaline, dopamine."},{"id":"L8-1ydk6nk","slide":248,"slides":[248],"q":"Which feature belongs to catecholamines?","o":["Rapid inactivation by MAO and COMT","Excellent penetration into the CNS tissue","Long half-lives of several hours","Low potency at all adrenoceptors"],"a":0,"e":"Catecholamines are potent, rapidly inactivated and penetrate the CNS poorly."},{"id":"L8-1n69424","slide":249,"slides":[249],"q":"Why do non-catecholamines such as ephedrine have longer half-lives?","o":["They resist COMT and are poor MAO substrates","They are more strongly bound to plasma albumin","They are never metabolised and leave unchanged","They are cleared only by hepatic metabolism"],"a":0,"e":"Without the catechol OH groups, they are not COMT substrates and are poor MAO substrates."},{"id":"L8-1v4noga","slide":250,"slides":[250],"q":"Which of these is an indirectly acting sympathomimetic?","o":["Isoprenaline","Phenylephrine","Tyramine","Noradrenaline"],"a":2,"e":"Amphetamine, cocaine and tyramine act indirectly; adrenaline and phenylephrine act directly."},{"id":"L8-1asrwxm","slide":250,"slides":[250],"q":"A drug stimulates adrenoceptors directly and also releases noradrenaline. What type is it?","o":["Direct-acting, such as isoprenaline","Indirect-acting, such as cocaine","Mixed-action, such as ephedrine","Uptake blocker, such as tyramine"],"a":2,"e":"Ephedrine, pseudoephedrine and metaraminol have mixed actions."},{"id":"L8-1ev2o7h","slide":250,"slides":[250],"q":"How does cocaine act as an indirect sympathomimetic?","o":["It binds directly to alpha-1 receptors","It blocks the uptake of noradrenaline","It blocks the release of noradrenaline","It blocks beta receptors"],"a":1,"e":"Cocaine is an uptake blocker, raising noradrenaline in the synapse."},{"id":"L8-1p49rrf","slide":251,"slides":[251],"q":"Which drug is a selective alpha-1 agonist?","o":["Isoprenaline","Dexmedetomidine","Terbutaline","Phenylephrine"],"a":3,"e":"Selective alpha-1 agonists include phenylephrine and oxymetazoline."},{"id":"L8-khjei","slide":251,"slides":[251],"q":"How does clonidine lower blood pressure?","o":["Alpha-1 stimulation causing marked arteriolar vasodilatation","Beta-1 blockade slowing the heart and cutting cardiac output","Alpha-2 stimulation, cutting release and acting centrally","Blocking the NET transporter in sympathetic nerve terminals"],"a":2,"e":"Alpha-2 agonists cause a fall in BP, partly by inhibiting noradrenaline release and partly centrally."},{"id":"L8-14eshu1","slide":252,"slides":[252],"q":"Which drug is a selective beta-1 agonist, and what may it cause?","o":["Salbutamol; paradoxical bronchospasm","Dobutamine; heart dysrhythmias","Salmeterol; marked vasoconstriction","Terbutaline; reflex bradycardia"],"a":1,"e":"Dobutamine increases contractility, but all beta-1 agonists can cause dysrhythmias."},{"id":"L8-8dj74v","slide":252,"slides":[252],"q":"Which drugs are selective beta-2 agonists used for their bronchodilator action?","o":["Dobutamine, phenylephrine, xamoterol","Clonidine, methyldopa, moxonidine","Atenolol, nebivolol, bisoprolol","Salbutamol, terbutaline, salmeterol"],"a":3,"e":"Salbutamol, terbutaline and salmeterol are used in asthma."},{"id":"L8-n6d9wu","slide":254,"slides":[254],"q":"Which drug is used in cardiac arrest and anaphylaxis?","o":["Dobutamine","Salbutamol","Adrenaline","Clonidine"],"a":2,"e":"Adrenaline is used in cardiac arrest and for anaphylaxis (acute hypersensitivity)."},{"id":"L8-13smvtd","slide":254,"slides":[254],"q":"Which drug is used in cardiogenic shock?","o":["Salbutamol","Dobutamine","Xylometazoline","Clonidine"],"a":1,"e":"Dobutamine, a beta-1 agonist, is used in cardiogenic shock."},{"id":"L8-rbsiij","slide":254,"slides":[254],"q":"Why is adrenaline added to some local anaesthetics?","o":["To speed their absorption","To make them lipid-soluble","To lower blood pressure","To prolong their action"],"a":3,"e":"Adrenaline vasoconstricts, slowing removal of the anaesthetic and prolonging its action."},{"id":"L8-ywc1r3","slide":254,"slides":[254],"q":"Which of these is a use of alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine?","o":["Relieving bronchospasm in acute asthma attacks","Treating cardiac arrest and cardiogenic shock","Delaying premature labour by relaxing the uterus","Lowering blood and intraocular pressure"],"a":3,"e":"Clonidine lowers BP and IOP and has other uses such as reducing menopausal flushing and migraine frequency."},{"id":"L8-1m4pkbz","slide":254,"slides":[254],"q":"Which drug is used to delay premature labour, and which receptor does it act on?","o":["Dobutamine, a beta-1 agonist","Phenylephrine, an alpha-1 agonist","Clonidine, an alpha-2 agonist","Salbutamol, a beta-2 agonist"],"a":3,"e":"Salbutamol is used in premature labour through beta-2 receptors."},{"id":"L8-1wpy50l","slide":257,"slides":[257],"q":"Which alpha-1-selective antagonist acts mainly on the urogenital tract?","o":["Prazosin","Tamsulosin","Phentolamine","Yohimbine"],"a":1,"e":"Tamsulosin is alpha-1A selective and acts mainly on the urogenital tract."},{"id":"L8-vxzsb1","slide":257,"slides":[257],"q":"Which unwanted effects are linked to alpha-1 antagonists such as prazosin?","o":["Bronchospasm and bradycardia","Postural hypotension and impotence","Hyperglycaemia and tremor","Constipation and urinary retention"],"a":1,"e":"Postural hypotension and impotence are the unwanted effects named."},{"id":"L8-p9p95k","slide":258,"slides":[258],"q":"Which drug is used to prepare a patient with phaeochromocytoma for surgery?","o":["Phenoxybenzamine","Isoprenaline","Dexmedetomidine","Phenylephrine"],"a":0,"e":"Phenoxybenzamine, an irreversible antagonist, is used in phaeochromocytoma."},{"id":"L8-x51mis","slide":258,"slides":[258],"q":"Which alpha-blocker is used for benign prostatic hypertrophy?","o":["Tamsulosin","Phenoxybenzamine","Yohimbine","Atenolol"],"a":0,"e":"Tamsulosin, an alpha-1A selective antagonist, is used in BPH."},{"id":"L8-46flpw","slide":260,"slides":[260],"q":"Which of these is a beta-1 selective antagonist?","o":["Atenolol","Propranolol","Oxprenolol","Labetalol"],"a":0,"e":"Atenolol and nebivolol are beta-1 selective; propranolol is non-selective."},{"id":"L8-11xvsl6","slide":260,"slides":[260],"q":"Which drugs block both alpha and beta adrenoceptors?","o":["Propranolol and atenolol","Prazosin and tamsulosin","Labetalol and carvedilol","Alprenolol and nebivolol"],"a":2,"e":"Labetalol and carvedilol block both alpha and beta receptors."},{"id":"L8-1j7wgl4","slide":260,"slides":[260],"q":"Which beta-blockers have partial agonist activity?","o":["Alprenolol and oxprenolol","Atenolol and nebivolol","Propranolol and carvedilol","Labetalol and atenolol"],"a":0,"e":"Alprenolol and oxprenolol have partial agonist activity."},{"id":"L8-19a9rpy","slide":261,"slides":[261],"q":"Why must non-selective beta-blockers be used with great care in asthma?","o":["They cause severe bronchodilatation","They raise heart rate and force sharply","They can cause bronchoconstriction","They cause severe urinary retention"],"a":2,"e":"Bronchoconstriction, bradycardia and cardiac failure are the important hazards."},{"id":"L8-1pveh50","slide":261,"slides":[261],"q":"Which side effects are listed for beta-blockers?","o":["Cold hands, insomnia, depression, fatigue","Tremor, tachycardia, sweating and anxiety","Dry mouth, constipation and blurred vision","Flushing, headache, hypotension, oedema"],"a":0,"e":"Side effects include cold extremities, insomnia, depression and fatigue."},{"id":"L8-1ntnb0w","slide":262,"slides":[262],"q":"Which beta-blocker eye drops are used for glaucoma?","o":["Timolol","Atenolol","Prazosin","Dobutamine"],"a":0,"e":"Timolol eye drops are listed for glaucoma."},{"id":"L8-1imq6l0","slide":262,"slides":[262],"q":"How can beta-blockers help in anxiety?","o":["By controlling somatic symptoms like tremor","By raising the heart rate to boost alertness","By blocking GABA receptors in the amygdala","By depleting noradrenaline stores centrally"],"a":0,"e":"They are used in anxiety to control somatic symptoms, not the mental symptoms."}]},{"id":"L9","title":"The ANS and Muscarinic Agonists","sub":"Autonomic anatomy, cholinergic transmission and cholinergic agonists","emoji":"🧠","slides":[265,305],"recap":["The ANS has sympathetic, parasympathetic and enteric divisions with a two-neuron pathway. Parasympathetic outflow is cranial (III, VII, IX, X) and sacral (S2-S4); sympathetic outflow is thoracolumbar.","Preganglionic neurons release ACh at nicotinic receptors; postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release ACh at muscarinic receptors; postganglionic sympathetic neurons are mostly noradrenergic (sweat glands are cholinergic).","ACh is made by ChAT, stored in vesicles, released by action potentials and broken down by AChE. M1 is neural, M2 cardiac, M3 glandular and smooth muscle.","Muscarinic agonists (bethanechol, pilocarpine, methacholine) cause bradycardia, secretions, smooth muscle contraction and miosis; pilocarpine lowers intraocular pressure in glaucoma."],"questions":[{"id":"L9-u60t7j","slide":269,"slides":[269],"q":"Which structures make up the central nervous system?","o":["The cranial and spinal nerves","The autonomic ganglia","The enteric plexuses","The brain and spinal cord"],"a":3,"e":"The CNS is the brain and spinal cord; the peripheral system is neurons outside them."},{"id":"L9-j1306f","slide":270,"slides":[270],"q":"Which three divisions make up the autonomic nervous system?","o":["Somatic, sympathetic and central divisions","Sensory, motor and enteric divisions of the CNS","Parasympathetic, somatic and spinal cord divisions","Sympathetic, parasympathetic and enteric"],"a":3,"e":"The ANS comprises sympathetic, parasympathetic and enteric divisions."},{"id":"L9-127zcuh","slide":270,"slides":[270],"q":"In the two-neuron autonomic pathway, where does the postganglionic cell body lie?","o":["In the spinal cord","In an autonomic ganglion","In the brain stem","In the target organ's smooth muscle"],"a":1,"e":"The preganglionic cell body is in the CNS; the postganglionic cell body is in an autonomic ganglion."},{"id":"L9-1ofxfu8","slide":271,"slides":[271],"q":"Which cranial nerves carry parasympathetic outflow?","o":["III, VII, IX and X","I, II, IV and VI","V, VIII, XI and XII","II, V, VII and XII"],"a":0,"e":"Parasympathetic outflow is via cranial nerves III, VII, IX, X and sacral S2-S4."},{"id":"L9-198focb","slide":271,"slides":[271],"q":"Where does sympathetic outflow leave the CNS?","o":["Cranial nerves and sacral spinal roots","Cervical and sacral spinal roots only","The brain stem and cranial nerves only","Thoracic and lumbar spinal roots"],"a":3,"e":"Sympathetic outflow is thoracolumbar."},{"id":"L9-zc65r9","slide":271,"slides":[271],"q":"Where does the enteric nervous system lie, and what can it do?","o":["In the spinal cord; it needs the brain for control","In GI intramural plexuses; it can act on its own","In the sympathetic chain; it only relays signals","In the adrenal medulla; it only secretes hormones"],"a":1,"e":"The enteric system lies in the intramural plexuses and can control motor and secretory functions independently."},{"id":"L9-1x09k4l","slide":272,"slides":[272],"q":"In the diagram, which structure carries sacral parasympathetic outflow to the pelvic ganglia?","o":["The paravertebral chain","The nervi erigentes","The prevertebral ganglia","The adrenal medulla"],"a":1,"e":"The diagram labels the nervi erigentes and pelvic ganglia in the sacral parasympathetic outflow."},{"id":"L9-yx2agd","slide":273,"slides":[273],"q":"When does sympathetic activity increase, and when does parasympathetic activity predominate?","o":["Sympathetic during rest and digestion; parasympathetic in stress","Sympathetic in stress; parasympathetic in satiation and repose","Both divisions are active only during sleep and rest","Sympathetic after meals and rest; parasympathetic in exercise"],"a":1,"e":"Sympathetic activity rises in fight or flight; parasympathetic activity prevails during satiation and repose."},{"id":"L9-1jea30z","slide":273,"slides":[273],"q":"In which organs do the two divisions NOT have opposing actions?","o":["Heart and gastrointestinal smooth muscle","Heart and bronchi only","Bladder and gut only","Salivary glands and ciliary muscle"],"a":3,"e":"They oppose each other in heart rate and GI muscle, but not in salivary glands or ciliary muscle."},{"id":"L9-1ek5v0f","slide":276,"slides":[276],"q":"How are autonomic neurons classified in this lecture?","o":["By the length of their axons and fibre diameter","By the organ they innervate and its location","By their cell body size and conduction speed","By the neurotransmitter they release"],"a":3,"e":"Neurons are cholinergic or (nor)adrenergic, based on their neurotransmitter."},{"id":"L9-1mdnqbs","slide":277,"slides":[277],"q":"Which receptor mediates transmission at autonomic ganglia?","o":["Nicotinic ACh receptors","Muscarinic ACh receptors","Beta-adrenoceptors","Alpha-adrenoceptors"],"a":0,"e":"Preganglionic neurons are cholinergic and ganglionic transmission occurs through nicotinic ACh receptors."},{"id":"L9-1d8eho1","slide":277,"slides":[277],"q":"At target organs, parasympathetic postganglionic neurons act on which receptors?","o":["Nicotinic receptors of the ganglionic type","Muscarinic receptors","Beta-adrenoceptors on the effector cells","Dopamine receptors on the effector organ"],"a":1,"e":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons are cholinergic and act on muscarinic receptors."},{"id":"L9-1bzlpwq","slide":278,"slides":[278],"q":"Which sympathetic postganglionic structures are cholinergic?","o":["Heart","Blood vessels of skeletal muscle only","Sweat glands","Salivary glands only"],"a":2,"e":"A few sympathetic postganglionic neurons, e.g. to sweat glands, are cholinergic."},{"id":"L9-11kp93y","slide":278,"slides":[278],"q":"Which NANC transmitters are listed for the parasympathetic system?","o":["ATP and neuropeptide Y","GABA and dopamine","Nitric oxide and VIP","Serotonin and histamine"],"a":2,"e":"NO and VIP are parasympathetic; ATP and neuropeptide Y are sympathetic."},{"id":"L9-92fbfx","slide":281,"slides":[281],"q":"The parasympathetic system mainly regulates which functions?","o":["The fight-or-flight response and stress adaptation","Housekeeping functions such as digestion and excretion","Skeletal muscle contraction and voluntary movement","Voluntary reflexes of the limbs and posture"],"a":1,"e":"It regulates digestion, excretion, temperature regulation and bladder contraction."},{"id":"L9-1vxrty7","slide":282,"slides":[282],"q":"Which arrangement of fibres is typical of the parasympathetic system?","o":["Short preganglionic, long postganglionic","Long preganglionic and long postganglionic","Only postganglionic fibres","Long preganglionic, short postganglionic"],"a":3,"e":"The ganglia lie near or within the organ, so postganglionic fibres are short."},{"id":"L9-12m01id","slide":283,"slides":[283],"q":"What is the parasympathetic effect on the heart?","o":["Increased heart rate","Decreased heart rate","Increased force of contraction","No effect"],"a":1,"e":"Parasympathetic stimulation slows the heart."},{"id":"L9-15kx7bq","slide":283,"slides":[283],"q":"What is the parasympathetic effect on the eye?","o":["Mydriasis and relaxation of accommodation","Mydriasis and accommodation","Miosis and accommodation","Miosis and loss of accommodation"],"a":2,"e":"Pupil constriction and accommodation occur."},{"id":"L9-19pjzlj","slide":283,"slides":[283],"q":"What is the parasympathetic effect on the bladder?","o":["Detrusor relaxation and increased capacity","Sphincter contraction and urinary retention","Reduced ureteral peristalsis","Detrusor contraction and decreased capacity"],"a":3,"e":"Stimulation contracts the detrusor, raises voiding pressure and lowers bladder capacity."},{"id":"L9-161kiwm","slide":283,"slides":[283],"q":"What are the parasympathetic effects on the gut?","o":["Decreased tone and secretions, leading to constipation","Reduced motility and sphincter tightening with dry mouth","Increased tone and peristalsis, with possible cramps","No change in motility, only a rise in gastric secretion"],"a":2,"e":"Motility and secretions rise, and may cause nausea, belching, cramps and diarrhoea."},{"id":"L9-4w408x","slide":285,"slides":[285],"q":"Which enzyme makes acetylcholine, and from what?","o":["Acetylcholinesterase, from choline and acetic acid","Choline acetyltransferase, from choline and acetyl-CoA","Tyrosine hydroxylase, from tyrosine and oxygen","DOPA decarboxylase, from DOPA and pyridoxal phosphate"],"a":1,"e":"ChAT converts choline and acetyl-CoA to ACh in the nerve terminal."},{"id":"L9-d6gj3a","slide":286,"slides":[286],"q":"How is the action of ACh at the synapse terminated?","o":["Reuptake of intact ACh by the noradrenaline transporter","Metabolism by MAO and COMT within the nerve terminal","AChE breaks it down into choline and acetate","Diffusion into the plasma and excretion unchanged"],"a":2,"e":"Acetylcholinesterase hydrolyses ACh into choline and acetate."},{"id":"L9-yswv8a","slide":286,"slides":[286],"q":"How is choline recycled after ACh breakdown?","o":["By being excreted in urine after hydrolysis in plasma","By conversion to noradrenaline within the vesicles","By a sodium-dependent choline transporter into the terminal","By being stored extracellularly in the synaptic cleft for reuse"],"a":2,"e":"Choline is taken up by a sodium-dependent transporter for further ACh synthesis."},{"id":"L9-1s50kog","slide":288,"slides":[288],"q":"Which muscarinic receptor mediates a decrease in cardiac rate and force?","o":["M2 ('cardiac')","M1 ('neural')","M3 ('glandular')","Nicotinic receptor"],"a":0,"e":"M2 receptors slow the heart, mainly the atria."},{"id":"L9-1estvka","slide":288,"slides":[288],"q":"Which effects are linked to M3 receptors?","o":["Slow ganglionic excitation with neuronal depolarisation","Decreased cardiac rate and reduced atrial force","Secretion, visceral muscle contraction and vasodilatation","Skeletal muscle contraction and fasciculation at the end plate"],"a":2,"e":"M3 ('glandular') produces secretion, contraction of visceral smooth muscle and vascular relaxation."},{"id":"L9-q800rv","slide":288,"slides":[288],"q":"Which muscarinic receptor produces slow excitation of ganglia?","o":["M2 ('cardiac')","M3 ('glandular')","Muscle nicotinic receptor","M1 ('neural')"],"a":3,"e":"M1 receptors are the neural type giving slow ganglionic excitation."},{"id":"L9-16clw1q","slide":291,"slides":[291],"q":"In the muscarinic receptor diagram, which signalling is linked to M2/M4 receptors?","o":["Stimulation of phospholipase C","Opening of ion channels directly","Inhibition of adenylate cyclase","Stimulation of adenylate cyclase"],"a":2,"e":"M2/M4 inhibit adenylate cyclase; M1/M3/M5 stimulate phospholipase C."},{"id":"L9-qmoisk","slide":293,"slides":[293],"q":"How do anticholinesterases enhance cholinergic transmission?","o":["They prevent destruction of endogenous ACh","They block postsynaptic ACh receptors on the effector","They stop ACh synthesis in the nerve terminal","They block choline uptake into the terminal"],"a":0,"e":"Drugs can act on receptors or affect the release or destruction of endogenous ACh; anticholinesterases stop its destruction."},{"id":"L9-n92klj","slide":294,"slides":[294],"q":"Which pair of drug groups acts at the autonomic ganglia?","o":["Muscarinic receptor agonists and antagonists only","Depolarising and non-depolarising muscle blockers","Anticholinesterases and ACh release stimulant drugs","Ganglion-stimulating and ganglion-blocking drugs"],"a":3,"e":"The groups are listed as drugs affecting muscarinic receptors, ganglia, neuromuscular transmission and those enhancing transmission."},{"id":"L9-1vrq51s","slide":295,"slides":[295],"q":"Which drug is a naturally occurring alkaloid muscarinic agonist?","o":["Pilocarpine","Bethanechol","Carbachol","Methacholine"],"a":0,"e":"Pilocarpine, muscarine and arecoline are alkaloids; the others are choline esters."},{"id":"L9-1pql6r9","slide":295,"slides":[295],"q":"How do the directly acting muscarinic agonists compare with ACh?","o":["They have shorter durations of action","They have longer durations of action","They act only indirectly","They block ACh receptors"],"a":1,"e":"All are directly acting with longer durations than acetylcholine."},{"id":"L9-14aud8p","slide":298,"slides":[298],"q":"What happens to blood pressure with a muscarinic agonist?","o":["It rises because of intense vasoconstriction and tachycardia","It falls because of bradycardia and vasodilatation","It stays the same since vagal and vascular effects cancel","It rises because of reflex tachycardia and raised output"],"a":1,"e":"Endothelium-dependent vasodilatation and bradycardia lower blood pressure."},{"id":"L9-7xyvmu","slide":298,"slides":[298],"q":"Why can muscarinic agonists interfere with breathing?","o":["They paralyse the diaphragm and intercostal muscles","They block alveolar gas exchange across the membrane","Bronchial secretion and constriction combine","They cause laryngeal relaxation and airway collapse"],"a":2,"e":"The combined effect of bronchial secretion and constriction can interfere with breathing."},{"id":"L9-15cra3k","slide":298,"slides":[298],"q":"What is the effect of muscarinic agonists on the eye?","o":["Pupillary constriction and ciliary contraction, lowering IOP","Pupil dilatation and relaxation of the ciliary muscle, raising IOP","Pupil dilatation and paralysis of accommodation, lowering IOP","Pupil constriction and ciliary spasm, raising intraocular pressure"],"a":0,"e":"Miosis and ciliary muscle contraction decrease intraocular pressure."},{"id":"L9-1cgs9cg","slide":299,"slides":[299],"q":"Why does bethanechol last longer than acetylcholine?","o":["It is not rapidly hydrolysed by AChE","It is rapidly excreted unchanged by the kidneys","It is bound tightly to plasma proteins in blood","It increases acetylcholine release from the terminals"],"a":0,"e":"Bethanechol is a carbamic ester resistant to rapid hydrolysis by AChE, with strong muscarinic and little nicotinic action."},{"id":"L9-1a6323w","slide":300,"slides":[300],"q":"What is a therapeutic use of bethanechol?","o":["Stimulating the atonic bladder","Dilating the pupil before eye examination","Treating bronchospasm in acute asthma","Preventing motion sickness on journeys"],"a":0,"e":"Bethanechol is used for atonic bladder and atony of the GIT."},{"id":"L9-bpz8hf","slide":300,"slides":[300],"q":"Which adverse effects result from generalised cholinergic stimulation by bethanechol?","o":["Dry mouth, constipation, tachycardia and urinary retention","Dilated pupils, urinary retention, dry skin and tachycardia","Hypertension, insomnia, tremor, tachycardia and dry mouth","Sweating, salivation, flushing, hypotension and bronchospasm"],"a":3,"e":"Bethanechol can cause sweating, salivation, flushing, lower BP, nausea, diarrhoea and bronchospasm."},{"id":"L9-1pngwy9","slide":301,"slides":[301],"q":"Which drug is a synthetic tertiary amine with strong CNS activity, used experimentally?","o":["Methacholine","Oxotremorine","Bethanechol","Pilocarpine"],"a":1,"e":"Oxotremorine strongly stimulates M receptors and is used experimentally."},{"id":"L9-1paxpiw","slide":301,"slides":[301],"q":"What is distinctive about methacholine?","o":["Strong muscarinic and cardiovascular effects; rarely used","A purely nicotinic action at ganglia; widely used in practice","A very short action with no cardiac effect; used for glaucoma","Mainly CNS effects, used in Alzheimer's dementia therapy"],"a":0,"e":"Methacholine has especially M effects, longer duration and profound cardiovascular effects, so it is rarely used."},{"id":"L9-6rih3b","slide":302,"slides":[302],"q":"Why can pilocarpine enter the CNS?","o":["It is a charged quaternary ammonium compound","It is a large, polar choline ester molecule","It is carried into the brain by albumin","It is an uncharged tertiary amine"],"a":3,"e":"Pilocarpine is uncharged and so penetrates the CNS at therapeutic doses."},{"id":"L9-1ryzavr","slide":303,"slides":[303],"q":"How does pilocarpine lower intraocular pressure in glaucoma?","o":["It blocks the production of aqueous humor by the ciliary body","It dilates the pupil and so widens the drainage angle","It paralyses the ciliary muscle to relax the lens","It opens Schlemm's canal so aqueous humor escapes"],"a":3,"e":"Pilocarpine causes miosis and ciliary contraction, opening Schlemm's canal."},{"id":"L9-ktyn16","slide":303,"slides":[303],"q":"What adverse effects arise when pilocarpine enters the CNS?","o":["Bradycardia only","Hyperglycaemia","CNS disturbances","Pupil dilatation"],"a":2,"e":"Once pilocarpine enters the CNS, CNS disturbances may follow."},{"id":"L9-1us4rbs","slide":304,"slides":[304],"q":"What does muscarine, found in mushrooms such as Amanita muscaria, cause?","o":["Profound muscarinic symptoms only","Nicotinic effects only","Both muscarinic and nicotinic effects","Sympathetic stimulation only"],"a":0,"e":"Muscarine has only M effects and can cause intoxication."},{"id":"L9-1fdqwgd","slide":304,"slides":[304],"q":"Why is arecoline not a first-line drug in Alzheimer's disease?","o":["It has no effect on memory","Possible carcinogenic properties","It acts only at nicotinic receptors","It cannot enter the CNS"],"a":1,"e":"Arecoline can improve memory but is not first-line because of possible carcinogenicity."},{"id":"L9-w3hplr","slide":305,"slides":[305],"q":"Which set of adverse effects is typical of muscarinic agonists?","o":["Tachycardia, dry mouth, constipation and urinary retention","Mydriasis, cycloplegia, blurred vision and photophobia","Hypertension, tremor, insomnia and increased appetite","Bradycardia, sweating, salivation and abdominal cramps"],"a":3,"e":"Muscarinic agonists cause bradycardia, flushing, sweating, salivation, nausea and abdominal cramps."},{"id":"L9-1e3nmy1","slide":305,"slides":[305],"q":"Which eye effects and respiratory effects are listed for muscarinic agonists?","o":["Mydriasis and cycloplegia; reduced secretions and bronchodilatation","Lacrimation and miosis; bronchial secretions and bronchoconstriction","Ptosis and mydriasis; dry airways with mild bronchodilatation","Blurred vision and dry eyes; thin secretions and bronchodilatation"],"a":1,"e":"The adverse effects include lacrimation, miosis, increased bronchial secretions and asthma attack."}]},{"id":"L10","title":"Muscarinic Antagonists","sub":"Atropine, scopolamine, ipratropium and other antimuscarinics","emoji":"🌿","slides":[306,324],"recap":["Antimuscarinics (atropine, scopolamine, ipratropium, pirenzepine) block muscarinic receptors, so parasympathetic effects are interrupted and sympathetic effects are left unopposed. They do not block nicotinic receptors.","Main effects: less secretion, tachycardia, mydriasis with cycloplegia, relaxed smooth muscle, and CNS effects (atropine excites; scopolamine sedates and causes amnesia).","Atropine is the prototype; scopolamine is the best anti-motion-sickness drug; ipratropium is an inhaled quaternary agent for asthma and COPD; tropicamide and cyclopentolate dilate the pupil for shorter periods.","Adverse effects: dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, blurred vision, raised intraocular pressure (careful in narrow-angle glaucoma), tachycardia and reduced sweating."],"questions":[{"id":"L10-2jcxjx","slide":307,"slides":[307],"q":"What do cholinergic antagonists do at cholinoceptors?","o":["Bind to them and trigger a stronger-than-normal response","Occupy them without triggering the usual effects","Destroy acetylcholine before it reaches them","Block the release of acetylcholine from the nerve terminal"],"a":1,"e":"Antagonists occupy the receptor but do not trigger receptor-mediated effects."},{"id":"L10-2pg0da","slide":307,"slides":[307],"q":"What follows when muscarinic synapses are blocked?","o":["Sympathetic effects stop and parasympathetic effects go unopposed","Both sympathetic and parasympathetic effects stop equally","Parasympathetic effects stop and sympathetic effects go unopposed","Both systems become overactive together"],"a":2,"e":"Blocking the parasympathetic muscarinic synapses leaves sympathetic stimulation unopposed."},{"id":"L10-1fk3ltl","slide":308,"slides":[308],"q":"Which group of cholinergic blockers is clinically the least important?","o":["Antimuscarinic drugs","Ganglionic blockers","Neuromuscular blockers","Anticholinesterases"],"a":1,"e":"Ganglionic blockers prefer nicotinic receptors of autonomic ganglia and are the least clinically useful."},{"id":"L10-ptbv0m","slide":308,"slides":[308],"q":"Neuromuscular blocking agents are mainly used for what?","o":["To treat bronchial asthma in emergency settings","To lower intraocular pressure in chronic glaucoma","As adjuvants in anaesthesia during surgery","To prevent motion sickness during long journeys"],"a":2,"e":"They interfere with efferent impulses to skeletal muscle and are used as anaesthetic adjuvants."},{"id":"L10-1bhp7xl","slide":310,"slides":[310],"q":"Which of these is an antimuscarinic agent?","o":["Bethanechol","Pirenzepine","Pilocarpine","Methacholine"],"a":1,"e":"Atropine, scopolamine, ipratropium and pirenzepine are antimuscarinics."},{"id":"L10-11occ0i","slide":310,"slides":[310],"q":"Why do antimuscarinics also reduce salivation and sweating?","o":["They block noradrenaline reuptake in the secretory glands","They stimulate alpha-2 receptors on the glandular acini","They block the few sympathetic neurons that are cholinergic","They cause the gland cells to atrophy within hours"],"a":2,"e":"Sympathetic neurons supplying salivary and sweat glands are cholinergic and so are blocked."},{"id":"L10-1gp2y77","slide":310,"slides":[310],"q":"How do cholinergic blockers compare with cholinergic agonists in usefulness?","o":["Agonists are useful in many situations; blockers have limited use","Both are equally limited, with few clinical situations of use","Neither group has any therapeutic use outside anaesthesia","Blockers have many clinical uses; agonists have limited use"],"a":3,"e":"Cholinergic agonists have limited therapeutic usefulness, while blockers are beneficial in a variety of situations."},{"id":"L10-35av0m","slide":311,"slides":[311],"q":"Why do antimuscarinics have little action at the skeletal neuromuscular junction?","o":["They are destroyed by cholinesterase at the junction","They cannot reach the muscle from the bloodstream","They do not block nicotinic receptors","They act only on the heart and gastrointestinal tract"],"a":2,"e":"Neuromuscular and ganglionic transmission use nicotinic receptors, which antimuscarinics do not block."},{"id":"L10-rub50a","slide":312,"slides":[312],"q":"Which pair of effects is typical of antimuscarinic drugs?","o":["Bradycardia and pupil constriction","Increased secretions and diarrhoea","Tachycardia and pupil dilatation","Bronchoconstriction and salivation"],"a":2,"e":"Antimuscarinics cause tachycardia, mydriasis and paralysis of accommodation, plus reduced secretions."},{"id":"L10-nuvvsm","slide":312,"slides":[312],"q":"Which antimuscarinic is especially associated with reduced gastric acid secretion?","o":["Atropine","Scopolamine","Pirenzepine","Ipratropium"],"a":2,"e":"Pirenzepine, an M1 antagonist, inhibits gastric acid secretion."},{"id":"L10-1ggkmki","slide":312,"slides":[312],"q":"How do the CNS effects of atropine and scopolamine differ?","o":["Atropine is depressant with amnesia; scopolamine is excitatory","Both are purely excitatory, causing seizures and tremor","Atropine excites; scopolamine depresses, with amnesia","Neither has any CNS effect as both are quaternary amines"],"a":2,"e":"The slide lists excitatory effects with atropine and depressant effects including amnesia with scopolamine."},{"id":"L10-1ooxf82","slide":313,"slides":[313],"q":"Which adverse effect is highlighted on the adverse-effects slide for cholinergic antagonists?","o":["Urinary urgency","Bradycardia","Urinary retention","Excessive salivation"],"a":2,"e":"Urinary retention is the effect shown, as the detrusor relaxes."},{"id":"L10-1bq7x60","slide":314,"slides":[314],"q":"What is atropine, and where does it come from?","o":["A tertiary belladonna alkaloid from Atropa belladonna","A quaternary amine alkaloid from the Datura plant seeds","A synthetic choline ester derived from carbamic acid","A polypeptide neurotoxin purified from snake venom"],"a":0,"e":"Atropine is a tertiary amine belladonna alkaloid obtained from deadly nightshade."},{"id":"L10-1c79b29","slide":314,"slides":[314],"q":"How does atropine bind muscarinic receptors?","o":["Irreversibly, covalently destroying the receptor protein","Competitively, preventing acetylcholine from binding","By increasing acetylcholine release from nerve endings","By blocking acetylcholine synthesis in the terminal"],"a":1,"e":"Atropine has high affinity and binds competitively, keeping ACh away."},{"id":"L10-1byqvj","slide":314,"slides":[314],"q":"Where does atropine act, and how long do its general actions last?","o":["Peripherally only; about 24 hours in most tissues","Centrally only; roughly 1 hour at therapeutic doses","Peripherally only; about 12 hours in all tissues","Centrally and peripherally; about 4 hours"],"a":3,"e":"It acts both centrally and peripherally, with general actions lasting about 4 hours (longer in the eye)."},{"id":"L10-3a7euf","slide":315,"slides":[315],"q":"Which set describes the effect of atropine on the eye?","o":["Miosis, a brisk light response and active accommodation","Mydriasis with a normal light response and accommodation","Miosis with a poor light response and loss of near vision","Persistent mydriasis, no light response and cycloplegia"],"a":3,"e":"Atropine causes persistent mydriasis, unresponsiveness to light and cycloplegia."},{"id":"L10-1ll7vt1","slide":315,"slides":[315],"q":"Why is atropine risky in narrow-angle glaucoma?","o":["Intraocular pressure falls too far, damaging the retina","Intraocular pressure may rise dangerously","It causes permanent miosis and ciliary spasm","It damages the optic nerve by direct toxicity"],"a":1,"e":"Pupil dilation can obstruct drainage and raise IOP in narrow-angle glaucoma."},{"id":"L10-vstd88","slide":315,"slides":[315],"q":"Which agents are generally preferred to dilate the pupil in eye examinations?","o":["Tropicamide or phenylephrine","Pilocarpine or bethanechol drops","Timolol or atenolol eye drops","Neostigmine or physostigmine drops"],"a":0,"e":"Shorter-acting tropicamide or the alpha agonist phenylephrine are favoured over atropine."},{"id":"L10-oycd8","slide":316,"slides":[316],"q":"What is atropine's effect on the gastrointestinal tract?","o":["It acts as an antispasmodic, reducing GI activity","It raises GI tone and peristalsis, causing colic pain","It causes diarrhoea through faster intestinal transit","It strongly increases gastric acid and mucus secretion"],"a":0,"e":"Atropine and scopolamine are potent antispasmodics."},{"id":"L10-1344ge2","slide":316,"slides":[316],"q":"Why is atropine not effective for healing peptic ulcers?","o":["It raises acid production and worsens mucosal injury","It cannot reach the gastric mucosa after oral dosing","It cuts motility but barely reduces acid production","It destroys the protective mucus layer of the stomach"],"a":2,"e":"Hydrochloric acid production is not significantly affected."},{"id":"L10-85ehnb","slide":317,"slides":[317],"q":"Scopolamine is also known as:","o":["Pilocarpine","Tropicamide","Pirenzepine","Hyoscine"],"a":3,"e":"Scopolamine, levo-duboisine and hyoscine are the same drug, from Datura stramonium."},{"id":"L10-s7njyb","slide":317,"slides":[317],"q":"How does scopolamine differ from atropine?","o":["Weaker CNS action at usual doses and a shorter duration","No peripheral effects, acting only within the brain","It is a quaternary amine that cannot enter the CNS","More CNS action at usual doses, and longer lasting"],"a":3,"e":"Scopolamine produces CNS effects at therapeutic doses and lasts longer."},{"id":"L10-108b8qy","slide":318,"slides":[318],"q":"Which of these is scopolamine most effective for?","o":["Treating bronchospasm in asthma","Raising heart rate in cardiogenic shock","Preventing motion sickness","Lowering blood pressure in hypertension"],"a":2,"e":"It is one of the most effective anti-motion-sickness drugs."},{"id":"L10-1icsyha","slide":318,"slides":[318],"q":"Which unusual CNS effect does scopolamine produce?","o":["Improvement of short-term memory","Increased appetite","Blockade of short-term memory","Permanent sedation"],"a":2,"e":"Scopolamine blocks short-term memory."},{"id":"L10-zv0ku4","slide":318,"slides":[318],"q":"What happens when scopolamine is given at higher doses?","o":["It can produce excitement instead of sedation","It always deepens sedation","It stops all CNS activity","It makes the pupils constrict"],"a":0,"e":"Scopolamine normally sedates, but at higher doses it may excite; it can also cause euphoria and be abused."},{"id":"L10-5z2hcy","slide":319,"slides":[319],"q":"When is an anti-motion-sickness drug most effective?","o":["Once vomiting is established","Only after the journey ends","Prophylactically, before motion starts","Only when given by injection"],"a":2,"e":"Such drugs are far more effective prophylactically than for treating established motion sickness."},{"id":"L10-nahjy6","slide":319,"slides":[319],"q":"Why is scopolamine useful in anaesthetic procedures?","o":["Its stimulant action on the heart","Its ability to constrict the pupil","Its amnesic action","Its action as a local anaesthetic"],"a":2,"e":"The amnesic action makes scopolamine an important adjunct."},{"id":"L10-hc21jr","slide":320,"slides":[320],"q":"Why does inhaled ipratropium not enter the systemic circulation or CNS?","o":["It is a tertiary amine with no charge","It is too large to cross any membrane","It is bound to albumin in the lung","It is a quaternary derivative with a positive charge"],"a":3,"e":"Its positive charge keeps its effects confined to the lungs."},{"id":"L10-57apst","slide":320,"slides":[320],"q":"Ipratropium is useful in asthma for which patients?","o":["Those who cannot inhale medicine","Those unable to take adrenergic agonists","Those who have glaucoma","Those with a slow heart rate"],"a":1,"e":"It is used in asthma patients who cannot take adrenergic agonists, and in COPD."},{"id":"L10-ul1115","slide":321,"slides":[321],"q":"Which pair of mydriatic durations is correct?","o":["Tropicamide 24 hours; cyclopentolate 6 hours","Tropicamide 6 hours; cyclopentolate 24 hours","Both 6 hours","Both 24 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Tropicamide produces mydriasis for 6 h and cyclopentolate for 24 h, both shorter than atropine."},{"id":"L10-15dtuws","slide":323,"slides":[323],"q":"Which set lists adverse effects of muscarinic antagonists?","o":["Dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention and blurred vision","Salivation, diarrhoea, bradycardia and miosis","Sweating, flushing, hypotension and lacrimation","Bronchospasm, miosis and abdominal cramps"],"a":0,"e":"Antimuscarinics cause dry mouth, constipation, urine retention, blurred vision and dilated pupils."},{"id":"L10-4n5n8a","slide":323,"slides":[323],"q":"Which skin effect is listed for muscarinic antagonists?","o":["Excessive sweating","Flushing with sweating","Decreased sweating or anhidrosis","Cold clammy skin from vasoconstriction"],"a":2,"e":"Decreased sweating or anhidrosis, with urticaria and other dermal effects."},{"id":"L10-1mxhdoa","slide":323,"slides":[323],"q":"Which cardiovascular effect is listed for muscarinic antagonists?","o":["Complete heart block","Orthostatic hypotension from vasodilatation","Palpitations and tachycardia","Cardiac arrest from bradycardia"],"a":2,"e":"CV effects are palpitations, tachycardia and sometimes paradoxical bradycardia."},{"id":"L10-1lxwaex","slide":324,"slides":[324],"q":"Which drug treats sinus bradycardia, for example after myocardial infarction?","o":["Tropicamide","Atropine","Hyoscine","Pirenzepine"],"a":1,"e":"Atropine is used for sinus bradycardia."},{"id":"L10-w1s0kb","slide":324,"slides":[324],"q":"Which drugs are given by inhalation for asthma and COPD?","o":["Hyoscine or dicycloverine","Benztropine or benzhexol","Tropicamide or cyclopentolate","Ipratropium or tiotropium"],"a":3,"e":"Ipratropium and tiotropium are inhaled antimuscarinics."},{"id":"L10-1qyn6gr","slide":324,"slides":[324],"q":"Which drugs counter movement disorders caused by antipsychotics?","o":["Atropine and ipratropium","Hyoscine and tropicamide","Dicycloverine and pirenzepine","Benzhexol and benztropine"],"a":3,"e":"Antimuscarinics such as benzhexol and benztropine are used in parkinsonism."},{"id":"L10-1j6vyk4","slide":324,"slides":[324],"q":"Why might hyoscine be given before endoscopy?","o":["It relaxes gastrointestinal smooth muscle","It stimulates gastric emptying","It constricts the pupil","It raises blood pressure"],"a":0,"e":"Antispasmodic action facilitates endoscopy and GI radiology."},{"id":"L10-siti6a","slide":324,"slides":[324],"q":"Dicycloverine (dicyclomine) is used as an antispasmodic in which conditions?","o":["Asthma and COPD","Glaucoma and uveitis","Irritable bowel syndrome and colonic diverticular disease","Sinus bradycardia and heart block"],"a":2,"e":"It is listed for IBS and diverticular disease."},{"id":"L10-qckk5m","slide":324,"slides":[324],"q":"Which M1-selective antagonist is used for peptic ulcer disease?","o":["Ipratropium","Hyoscine","Pirenzepine","Tropicamide"],"a":2,"e":"Pirenzepine suppresses gastric acid secretion."},{"id":"L10-boh2v5","slide":324,"slides":[324],"q":"Why are atropine and hyoscine used as anaesthetic premedication?","o":["To raise heart rate only","To dry secretions","To dilate the pupil","To cause amnesia only"],"a":1,"e":"They are given pre-operatively to dry secretions."}]},{"id":"L11","title":"Ganglia, Muscle Relaxants and Anticholinesterases","sub":"Ganglion drugs, neuromuscular blockers, cholinesterase inhibitors and pralidoxime","emoji":"🛡️","slides":[325,341],"recap":["Ganglion stimulants (nicotine, DMPP) have complex effects and no use except smoking cessation. Ganglion blockers (hexamethonium, trimetaphan) cause hypotension and are obsolete apart from trimetaphan for controlled hypotension.","Non-depolarising blockers (tubocurarine, atracurium, vecuronium) are competitive nicotinic antagonists reversed by neostigmine; suxamethonium depolarises, causes fasciculations and can cause hyperkalaemia, malignant hyperthermia and long paralysis in cholinesterase deficiency.","Anticholinesterases (neostigmine, pyridostigmine, edrophonium, donepezil, physostigmine, organophosphates) raise ACh. Uses: reversing blockers, myasthenia gravis, Alzheimer's disease, glaucoma. Poisoning gives cholinergic overactivity.","Pralidoxime reactivates cholinesterase if given before 'aging' and does not reach the CNS."],"questions":[{"id":"L11-9y1n5p","slide":325,"slides":[325],"q":"Which compounds are ganglion-stimulating drugs?","o":["Hexamethonium and trimetaphan","Nicotine and DMPP","Pilocarpine and bethanechol","Atropine and hyoscine"],"a":1,"e":"Nicotine and DMPP stimulate autonomic ganglia."},{"id":"L11-1ehtpzb","slide":325,"slides":[325],"q":"Why are the effects of ganglion stimulants complex?","o":["Only the parasympathetic ganglia are stimulated, with reflex changes","Only the sympathetic ganglia are stimulated, with rebound effects","The drugs act on skeletal muscle and on glands at the same time","Both sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia are stimulated"],"a":3,"e":"Stimulation of both divisions gives tachycardia, raised BP and variable GI effects."},{"id":"L11-5scdoe","slide":325,"slides":[325],"q":"Which effects follow ganglion stimulation?","o":["Bradycardia, low blood pressure and a dry mouth","Pupil constriction with reduced bronchial secretions","Tachycardia, raised BP and more secretions","Bronchodilatation with slowed gut movement and dry skin"],"a":2,"e":"Effects include tachycardia and BP rise, variable GI effects, and increased bronchial, salivary and sweat secretions."},{"id":"L11-jaukyg","slide":326,"slides":[326],"q":"What may follow ganglion stimulation?","o":["Depolarisation block","Permanent destruction of the ganglion cells","A lasting rise in the number of receptors","Direct inhibition of cholinesterase in the ganglion"],"a":0,"e":"Ganglion stimulation may be followed by depolarisation block."},{"id":"L11-1hxdljz","slide":326,"slides":[326],"q":"What is the only therapeutic use of nicotine mentioned?","o":["Treating high blood pressure in the elderly","Treating bronchial asthma attacks","Dilating the pupil for an eye examination","Helping people stop smoking"],"a":3,"e":"Ganglion stimulants have no therapeutic uses except nicotine to assist smoking cessation."},{"id":"L11-1ejs1rv","slide":327,"slides":[327],"q":"Which compounds are ganglion-blocking drugs?","o":["Nicotine and DMPP, which are ganglion stimulants","Neostigmine and edrophonium, the cholinesterase inhibitors","Atracurium and vecuronium, the neuromuscular blockers","Hexamethonium and trimetaphan"],"a":3,"e":"Hexamethonium, trimetaphan and tubocurarine block ganglia."},{"id":"L11-w92ifo","slide":327,"slides":[327],"q":"Which set lists main effects of ganglion blockers?","o":["Hypotension, loss of CV reflexes, GI paralysis and impaired micturition","Hypertension, tachycardia, diarrhoea and increased urination","Increased secretions, bronchospasm, miosis and salivation","Muscle fasciculations, paralysis, hyperkalaemia and fever"],"a":0,"e":"They block all autonomic and enteric ganglia."},{"id":"L11-fznje7","slide":327,"slides":[327],"q":"What is the remaining clinical use of trimetaphan?","o":["Treatment of acute glaucoma in the eye","Long-term treatment of myasthenia gravis","Reversal of neuromuscular blockade after surgery","Controlled hypotension during anaesthesia"],"a":3,"e":"Ganglion blockers are obsolete except occasional trimetaphan use for controlled hypotension."},{"id":"L11-1h80j7k","slide":328,"slides":[328],"q":"Which substance blocks choline uptake at the neuromuscular junction?","o":["Hemicholinium","Botulinum toxin","Neostigmine","Suxamethonium"],"a":0,"e":"Hemicholinium blocks choline uptake and is not used clinically."},{"id":"L11-172sh8i","slide":328,"slides":[328],"q":"Which agents block acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction?","o":["Tubocurarine and pancuronium given intravenously","Neostigmine and pyridostigmine given by mouth","Aminoglycosides and botulinum toxin","Nicotine and DMPP acting on the motor end plate"],"a":2,"e":"Aminoglycosides and botulinum toxin block ACh release."},{"id":"L11-9nz3sf","slide":328,"slides":[328],"q":"Non-depolarising blockers act as:","o":["Agonists that depolarise the nicotinic receptor","Inhibitors of the enzyme acetylcholinesterase","Blockers of the uptake of choline into the nerve","Competitive antagonists at nicotinic receptors"],"a":3,"e":"Tubocurarine, pancuronium, atracurium and vecuronium compete with ACh at nicotinic receptors."},{"id":"L11-c783fr","slide":328,"slides":[328],"q":"Which drug is the depolarising neuromuscular blocker named?","o":["Tubocurarine","Vecuronium","Pancuronium","Suxamethonium"],"a":3,"e":"Suxamethonium (succinylcholine) is the depolarising agent."},{"id":"L11-1s02k5w","slide":329,"slides":[329],"q":"How can a non-depolarising block be reversed?","o":["With anticholinesterase drugs","It cannot be reversed by any drug","By giving more of the same blocking drug","By giving a depolarising blocker in a large dose"],"a":0,"e":"Non-depolarising block is reversible by anticholinesterases; depolarising block is not."},{"id":"L11-1i0d06r","slide":329,"slides":[329],"q":"Which agent reverses the steroidal ('curonium') blockers?","o":["Neostigmine on its own","Atropine given intravenously","Suxamethonium in a small dose","Sugammadex"],"a":3,"e":"Steroidal 'curonium' drugs are reversed by sugammadex."},{"id":"L11-dh0agk","slide":329,"slides":[329],"q":"Which effects accompany depolarising block?","o":["Fasciculations first, often with muscle pain afterwards","Smooth paralysis with no twitching beforehand","Immediate relaxation, then rapid and pain-free recovery","Seizures at onset, followed by a high fever"],"a":0,"e":"Depolarising block produces initial fasciculations and often postoperative muscle pain."},{"id":"L11-tr4noi","slide":329,"slides":[329],"q":"Why can suxamethonium cause prolonged paralysis in some people?","o":["They carry an excess of acetylcholinesterase at the junction","They have additional nicotinic receptors on the muscle","They lack enough plasma cholinesterase","They are unable to excrete the drug through the kidneys"],"a":2,"e":"Suxamethonium is normally broken down by plasma cholinesterase; deficiency prolongs the effect."},{"id":"L11-rfka2","slide":330,"slides":[330],"q":"Which statement matches non-depolarising blockers in the comparison table?","o":["Fasciculations, then paralysis; reversed by neostigmine","Very rapid onset, with a very short duration of action","Paralysis without fasciculations; reversed by neostigmine","Agonists at nicotinic receptors causing persistent depolarisation"],"a":2,"e":"Non-depolarising blockers cause paralysis without fasciculations and are reversed by AChE inhibitors."},{"id":"L11-1li018l","slide":330,"slides":[330],"q":"Which statement matches suxamethonium in the comparison table?","o":["Slow onset, with a prolonged duration of action","Very rapid onset, very short duration","Variable duration, from short to long acting","Always reversed by neostigmine in every phase of block"],"a":1,"e":"Suxamethonium has a very rapid onset and a very short duration."},{"id":"L11-1vmrjrv","slide":330,"slides":[330],"q":"Rapid sequence intubation is a major use of which blocker?","o":["Rocuronium","Atracurium","Pancuronium","Succinylcholine"],"a":3,"e":"The table lists rapid sequence intubation and short procedures for the depolarising blocker."},{"id":"L11-j16iox","slide":330,"slides":[330],"q":"Which drug group is used for surgical muscle relaxation and mechanical ventilation?","o":["Depolarising blockers","Non-depolarising blockers","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Ganglion stimulants"],"a":1,"e":"The table lists these uses for non-depolarising blockers."},{"id":"L11-1t8pask","slide":331,"slides":[331],"q":"Which adverse effects are linked to tubocurarine?","o":["Ganglion block and histamine release, with hypotension and bronchoconstriction","Hyperkalaemia and malignant hyperthermia with raised intraocular pressure","Fasciculations and muscle pain with bradycardia on injection","Dysrhythmias from potassium release in burned or injured patients"],"a":0,"e":"Tubocurarine causes ganglion block and histamine release; newer agents have fewer side effects."},{"id":"L11-xf4461","slide":331,"slides":[331],"q":"Why may suxamethonium cause dysrhythmias in burned patients?","o":["Release of histamine","Release of potassium","Release of calcium","Block of the ganglia"],"a":1,"e":"K+ release, especially in burned or injured patients, can cause dysrhythmias."},{"id":"L11-1ceq831","slide":331,"slides":[331],"q":"Which rare adverse effect of suxamethonium is listed?","o":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Malignant hyperthermia","Kernicterus","Hepatic encephalopathy"],"a":1,"e":"Suxamethonium may rarely cause malignant hyperthermia, bradycardia and raised intraocular pressure."},{"id":"L11-2kg5sr","slide":332,"slides":[332],"q":"Drugs that enhance cholinergic transmission act mainly by:","o":["Blocking acetylcholine receptors","Blocking choline uptake","Inhibiting acetylcholine synthesis","Inhibiting cholinesterase"],"a":3,"e":"The main group works by inhibiting cholinesterase; others increase ACh release."},{"id":"L11-19g58xs","slide":333,"slides":[333],"q":"How does acetylcholinesterase differ from butyrylcholinesterase?","o":["AChE is membrane-bound and fairly specific for ACh; BuChE is non-selective","AChE is non-selective and found in plasma; BuChE is membrane-bound","AChE is a protease in the gut; BuChE is a lipase in the liver","Only BuChE hydrolyses acetylcholine rapidly at synapses"],"a":0,"e":"AChE rapidly hydrolyses ACh at cholinergic synapses; BuChE (pseudocholinesterase) is relatively non-selective."},{"id":"L11-m0qamg","slide":333,"slides":[333],"q":"Both cholinesterases belong to which enzyme family?","o":["Serine hydrolases","Cytochrome P450 enzymes","Glucuronyl transferases","Tyrosine kinases"],"a":0,"e":"Both are serine hydrolases."},{"id":"L11-1vo7bvd","slide":335,"slides":[335],"q":"How are anticholinesterases classified?","o":["Depolarising and non-depolarising","Reversible and irreversible","Direct-acting and indirect-acting","Tertiary and quaternary"],"a":1,"e":"The slide divides them into reversible anticholinesterases and irreversible organophosphates."},{"id":"L11-59idmi","slide":336,"slides":[336],"q":"Which autonomic effects follow anticholinesterase action?","o":["Tachycardia, hypertension, dry mouth and bronchodilatation","Mydriasis, cycloplegia, dry skin and urinary retention","Bradycardia, hypotension, excess secretions and bronchoconstriction","Constipation, urinary retention, dry eyes and tachycardia"],"a":2,"e":"Cholinergic transmission is enhanced, so parasympathetic effects appear, including GI hypermotility and lower IOP."},{"id":"L11-1ezt8al","slide":336,"slides":[336],"q":"Which anticholinesterases enter the CNS and cause marked central effects?","o":["Neostigmine and pyridostigmine","Physostigmine and organophosphates","Edrophonium and pralidoxime","Tubocurarine and atracurium"],"a":1,"e":"Those that cross the blood-brain barrier have marked CNS effects."},{"id":"L11-1thc8zs","slide":336,"slides":[336],"q":"What neuromuscular effects follow anticholinesterases?","o":["Fasciculation and more twitch tension, perhaps depolarisation block","Flaccid paralysis with loss of all twitch tension","No effect at the neuromuscular junction at all","Reduced twitch tension and prolonged muscle relaxation"],"a":0,"e":"Excess ACh at the junction causes fasciculation and may produce depolarisation block."},{"id":"L11-18rnhz5","slide":336,"slides":[336],"q":"Where might anticholinesterase poisoning come from?","o":["Antibiotics found in food","Insecticides or nerve gases","Plant alkaloids from belladonna","Heavy use of caffeine drinks"],"a":1,"e":"Exposure to insecticides or nerve gases may cause poisoning."},{"id":"L11-8ff584","slide":337,"slides":[337],"q":"Which drug reverses non-depolarising blockers at the end of an operation?","o":["Neostigmine, with atropine","Atropine used on its own","Suxamethonium given as a bolus","Pralidoxime given on its own"],"a":0,"e":"Atropine must accompany neostigmine to limit parasympathomimetic effects."},{"id":"L11-195l0kn","slide":337,"slides":[337],"q":"Which drugs treat myasthenia gravis?","o":["Atropine or hyoscine","Tubocurarine or atracurium","Suxamethonium or pancuronium","Neostigmine or pyridostigmine"],"a":3,"e":"Neostigmine and pyridostigmine are used in myasthenia gravis."},{"id":"L11-nr6za6","slide":337,"slides":[337],"q":"What does edrophonium help to distinguish?","o":["Narrow-angle glaucoma from open-angle glaucoma","Asthma attacks from chronic obstructive disease","Cholinergic crisis from myasthenic crisis","Angina from myocardial infarction in emergencies"],"a":2,"e":"Short-acting IV edrophonium tests for myasthenia and separates overdose weakness from disease weakness."},{"id":"L11-1nyoezv","slide":337,"slides":[337],"q":"Which anticholinesterase is named for Alzheimer's disease?","o":["Echothiophate","Pralidoxime","Neostigmine","Donepezil"],"a":3,"e":"Donepezil is used in Alzheimer's disease; echothiophate eye drops treat glaucoma."},{"id":"L11-s0wis2","slide":338,"slides":[338],"q":"What does pralidoxime do?","o":["Blocks the muscarinic receptors on target organs","Stimulates the release of acetylcholine from nerves","Reactivates inhibited cholinesterase","Blocks the nicotinic receptors at the muscle end plate"],"a":2,"e":"It displaces the phosphate group of the organophosphate and regenerates the enzyme."},{"id":"L11-1jpwizl","slide":338,"slides":[338],"q":"Why does pralidoxime not work in the CNS?","o":["It is broken down by brain enzymes on arrival","Its charged group prevents CNS entry","It is metabolised by the liver before reaching the CNS","It acts only on muscarinic receptors outside the brain"],"a":1,"e":"Its charge lets it approach the anionic site but prevents CNS penetration."},{"id":"L11-tm25nx","slide":339,"slides":[339],"q":"When is pralidoxime effective?","o":["Only once the enzyme has fully aged","If given before aging of the enzyme","Only against nerve agents that age within seconds","Only when it is swallowed rather than injected"],"a":1,"e":"Before aging it can reverse echothiophate's effects, except in the CNS."},{"id":"L11-1dbuhz3","slide":339,"slides":[339],"q":"Why is pralidoxime less effective with newer nerve agents?","o":["They are metabolised quickly by the liver","They bind only to muscarinic receptors in the brain","They are excreted faster than the older agents","They age the enzyme within seconds"],"a":3,"e":"Aging within seconds leaves little time for reactivation."},{"id":"L11-154oimx","slide":339,"slides":[339],"q":"What may high doses of pralidoxime cause?","o":["Complete reversal of every nerve agent","Cholinesterase-inhibitor side effects","A marked increase in pupil size and dry mouth","Loss of the sense of taste and smell"],"a":1,"e":"It is a weak AChE inhibitor and may cause similar side effects at higher doses."}]}]};
